/* QUANT TEST LAB — INVESTMENT BANKING TRACK Learn content.
   Same shape and depth standard as js/lessons.js (see concept 'c3' there for
   the reference bar). For finance concepts specifically, every primer goes
   beyond the formula: where the number comes from, what it means in
   practice, and what a high vs. low result implies for a company's health,
   risk or value — not just "how to compute it." track:'ib' on every unit. */
(function (global) {
  'use strict';

  const L = [

    /* =============================== IB-A =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-A', topic: 'Accounting Flows', title: 'How a transaction moves through the three financial statements', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'iba1', name: 'The three statements, and why one transaction touches all of them',
          primer: 'Before anything else, three terms need defining precisely, because interview and assessment questions live and die on confusing them. The "income statement" (also called the P&L, for "profit and loss") reports a company\'s revenue, its costs, and the profit left over, for a specific PERIOD of time (like a quarter or a year) — it answers "how much did the company earn during this period, on paper?" The "cash flow statement" reports how much actual CASH moved into and out of the company during that same period, split into three sections (operating, investing, financing) — it answers "how much real money changed hands?" and critically, it can differ substantially from the income statement, because accounting profit and cash are NOT the same thing (a sale can be recorded as revenue before the customer actually pays, for instance). The "balance sheet" is different in kind from the other two: instead of covering a period, it is a snapshot at one single INSTANT — it lists everything the company owns (assets), everything it owes (liabilities), and the difference between them (equity), always obeying the identity Assets = Liabilities + Equity at every single moment. The reason these three statements are always taught and tested TOGETHER, rather than separately, is that they are not independent — every single transaction a company makes shows up in more than one of them SIMULTANEOUSLY, because of how accounting is built: the income statement\'s bottom line (net income) flows directly into both the cash flow statement (as its starting point) and the balance sheet (added into retained earnings, part of equity); the cash flow statement\'s bottom line (the net change in cash) flows directly into the balance sheet\'s cash line. This is why the classic interview drill of "walk me through how a $10 depreciation charge affects all three statements" is really testing whether you understand this WIRING between the statements, not just one isolated formula.',
          core: 'The income statement asks "how much did we earn this period," the cash flow statement asks "how much cash actually moved," and the balance sheet is a snapshot of everything owned versus owed at one instant. They are wired together: net income flows into both cash flow and retained earnings; the cash flow statement\'s ending cash flows into the balance sheet.',
          when: 'Any "how does this transaction flow through the financials" question, or any question asking you to reconcile net income with cash flow or a balance sheet change.',
          formulas: ['Balance sheet identity, true at every instant: Assets = Liabilities + Equity.', 'Net income (from the income statement) is the starting line of the cash flow statement.', 'Ending cash (from the cash flow statement) becomes the cash balance on the next balance sheet; net income (net of dividends) flows into retained earnings, part of equity.'],
          intuition: 'Think of the income statement and cash flow statement as two different LENSES on the same underlying events — one measures economic performance on an accrual basis (was value created?), the other measures pure cash movement (did money actually move?) — and the balance sheet is the running scoreboard that both lenses ultimately feed into.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company makes a $100 cash sale, with $60 of cost of goods sold. Income statement: revenue +$100, cost −$60, so net income +$40 (ignoring tax for simplicity). Cash flow statement: since the sale was for cash, operating cash flow also increases, ultimately by $40 net (mirroring net income when there is no working-capital timing difference). Balance sheet: cash goes up by $40 (net effect after paying for the goods), and retained earnings (inside equity) goes up by $40, keeping Assets = Liabilities + Equity balanced.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A company makes a $100 sale ON CREDIT (the customer has not paid yet), with $60 of cost. Income statement: still shows revenue +$100, net income +$40 immediately — accounting recognises revenue when it is EARNED, not when cash is received. Cash flow statement: net income of +$40 flows in, but an increase in accounts receivable of $100 is subtracted (money is owed but not yet collected), giving operating cash flow of 40−100 = −$60 net cash effect from this sale alone (assuming the $60 cost was paid in cash). Balance sheet: accounts receivable (an asset) rises by $100, cash falls by $60 (from paying the cost), and retained earnings rises by $40 — check: ΔAssets = +100−60 = +40, which equals ΔEquity = +40. Balanced.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A company takes out a $200 bank loan. Income statement: NO effect at all — borrowing money is not revenue and creates no profit or loss. Cash flow statement: +$200 appears in the FINANCING section (not operating), since this is raising capital, not running the business. Balance sheet: cash (an asset) rises by $200, and debt (a liability) also rises by $200 — Assets and Liabilities move together by the same amount, so equity is completely unaffected, and the identity still balances (ΔAssets=+200=ΔLiabilities+ΔEquity(0)).' }
          ],
          trap: 'Assuming net income and cash flow must always be equal — they are usually different by design, precisely because of non-cash items (like depreciation) and timing differences (like a sale made on credit, or a cost paid in advance). A profitable company reporting positive net income can still run out of CASH if too much of its "profit" is trapped in unpaid receivables.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Which statement is a SNAPSHOT at one instant, rather than covering a period of time?', options: ['Income statement', 'Cash flow statement', 'Balance sheet', 'All three cover a period'], a: 2, why: 'The balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and equity as they stand at one specific moment; the other two statements cover a period.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'What is the balance sheet identity that must hold at every instant?', options: ['Revenue = Costs + Profit', 'Assets = Liabilities + Equity', 'Cash In = Cash Out', 'Net Income = Cash Flow'], a: 1, why: 'Everything a company owns (assets) is financed either by what it owes (liabilities) or by the owners\' stake (equity).' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A company makes a $50 sale entirely on credit (no cash received yet), with no costs. What happens on the balance sheet?', options: ['Cash rises by $50', 'Accounts receivable rises by $50, and retained earnings rises by $50', 'Nothing changes until the cash is collected', 'Liabilities rise by $50'], a: 1, why: 'Revenue is recognised immediately (raising retained earnings via net income), but since no cash was received, the offsetting asset is accounts receivable, not cash.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A company takes out a loan. What is the effect on net income?', options: ['Net income rises by the loan amount', 'Net income falls by the loan amount', 'No effect at all', 'It depends on the interest rate'], a: 2, why: 'Borrowing money is a financing activity, not revenue or an expense — it has zero direct effect on the income statement (only the resulting interest expense, in future periods, would).' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why can a profitable company (positive net income) still run out of cash?', options: ['This is impossible if net income is positive', 'Because "profit" and "cash" measure different things — profit can be tied up in unpaid receivables or inventory rather than converted to cash', 'Because taxes are always paid in cash', 'Because the balance sheet identity would be violated otherwise'], a: 1, why: 'Net income is an accrual concept; if too much revenue sits uncollected as receivables, or cash is tied up building inventory, a "profitable" company can still be cash-starved.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A company pays $30 cash for a cost that was already recorded as an expense last quarter (an accrued liability being settled). What happens THIS quarter?', options: ['Net income falls by $30 this quarter', 'No income statement effect this quarter; cash falls by $30 and the liability (accrued expense) falls by $30', 'Revenue falls by $30', 'Nothing happens on any statement'], a: 1, why: 'The expense already hit the income statement when it was ACCRUED (recognised); paying it later is a pure cash/liability event with no NEW income statement impact — this is the mirror image of the credit-sale example.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'iba2', name: 'Depreciation and the tax shield: why "non-cash" does not mean "irrelevant to cash"',
          primer: 'When a company buys a long-lived physical asset — a factory, a machine, a fleet of trucks — accounting rules do not let it record the entire purchase price as an expense in the year of purchase, because that would make the income statement misleading (a single huge expense in year one, then nothing in the many years the asset is actually used to generate revenue). Instead, the purchase cost is spread out over the asset\'s useful life as a series of smaller annual charges called "depreciation," each of which reduces reported profit a little at a time, matching the expense to the years the asset actually helps produce revenue — this accounting technique is called the "matching principle." The single most important, most frequently tested fact about depreciation is that it is a "non-cash" expense: the actual CASH for the asset was already paid out, in full, back when it was originally purchased (as an investing activity), so recording MORE depreciation charges in later years does not cause any FURTHER cash to leave the company at that time — the cash impact already happened. So what is depreciation\'s real effect on cash, if any? It comes entirely through taxes: because tax authorities allow depreciation to be deducted from taxable income (just like any other expense), a company that records more depreciation reports lower taxable profit, and therefore pays LESS in actual cash taxes than it would if depreciation were zero — this saving is called the "depreciation tax shield," and it equals the depreciation charge multiplied by the tax rate. This is precisely why, when building a cash flow statement from net income, accountants first SUBTRACT depreciation as an expense to get net income (following normal accounting rules), and then ADD IT BACK when computing cash flow — the add-back cancels out the artificial reduction to net income, but the reduced TAX BILL (which was genuinely paid in cash, calculated using the lower, depreciation-reduced taxable income) remains as a real cash benefit.',
          core: 'Depreciation itself moves no cash (the cash left when the asset was bought). Its only real cash effect is the "tax shield": by lowering taxable income, it lowers the actual cash tax bill by (depreciation × tax rate). On the cash flow statement, depreciation is added back to net income precisely to cancel out its artificial, non-cash reduction to reported profit.',
          when: 'Any "how does depreciation/amortisation affect net income, cash flow, or the balance sheet" question, and any DCF or LBO model where you must add back D&A to get from net income to cash flow.',
          formulas: ['Net income impact of a depreciation charge D at tax rate t = −D × (1 − t).', 'Cash flow impact of that same depreciation charge = +D × t (the tax shield only).', 'Cash flow build-up: Cash flow from operations = Net income + Depreciation & amortisation (add-back) + other non-cash items ± working-capital changes.'],
          intuition: 'A useful gut-check: if a $100 machine purchase is spread over 10 years as $10/year of depreciation, the $100 in CASH left the building the day the machine was bought — recording "$10 of depreciation" in year 5 is bookkeeping, not a fresh $10 leaving the bank account; only the resulting tax saving in year 5 is genuinely new cash.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company has $200 of pre-tax income before depreciation, then records $50 of depreciation, at a 25% tax rate. Taxable income = 200−50 = $150. Tax = 150×25% = $37.50. Net income = 150−37.50 = $112.50. Now compare to a hypothetical with ZERO depreciation: taxable income = $200, tax = $50, net income = $150. The depreciation REDUCED net income by 150−112.50 = $37.50, which is exactly $50×(1−25%) = $37.50, matching the formula.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Using the same numbers, what is the cash flow effect of that depreciation, versus the zero-depreciation scenario? With depreciation: cash tax paid = $37.50. Without depreciation: cash tax paid = $50. The company with depreciation pays $50−37.50 = $12.50 LESS in cash taxes — exactly the tax shield, $50×25% = $12.50. On the cash flow statement directly: net income of $112.50, plus the $50 depreciation add-back, gives operating cash flow of $162.50 — compare this to the no-depreciation net income of $150 (which, with no non-cash addback needed, is also its cash flow): $162.50 vs $150 is a $12.50 cash ADVANTAGE from having the depreciation deduction, confirming the tax shield once again.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A company\'s net PP&E (property, plant & equipment) was $500 at the start of the year. During the year it spent $80 on capital expenditures (buying new equipment) and recorded $60 of depreciation. What is net PP&E at year end, and does this affect cash directly? Net PP&E rolls forward as: starting balance + capex − depreciation = 500+80−60 = $520. The capex of $80 is a genuine NEW cash outflow this year (shown in the investing section of the cash flow statement); the $60 of depreciation, being a reduction of an asset ALREADY paid for, causes no new direct cash outflow — its only cash effect is, again, the tax shield on the income statement side.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Treating depreciation as if it reduces cash flow by its full amount, the same way it reduces net income — this conflates a non-cash accounting charge with an actual cash expense. The correct cash effect of depreciation is ONLY the tax shield (depreciation × tax rate), and it is a cash BENEFIT (it lowers the tax bill), not a cash cost.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Depreciation is described as a "non-cash" expense because:', options: ['It never appears on any financial statement', 'The cash for the underlying asset was already spent when it was purchased; the depreciation charge itself moves no new cash', 'Depreciation is illegal to deduct from taxes', 'It only appears on the balance sheet'], a: 1, why: 'The cash outflow happened at the original purchase (an investing activity); the subsequent depreciation charges are a bookkeeping allocation of that already-spent cash, not new cash leaving the company.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'On the cash flow statement, depreciation is:', options: ['Subtracted from net income', 'Added back to net income', 'Ignored entirely', 'Multiplied by the tax rate before being used at all'], a: 1, why: 'Since depreciation already reduced net income under normal accounting rules but involves no new cash, it is added back to reconstruct the actual cash flow.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A $40 depreciation charge at a 30% tax rate. What is the net income impact?', options: ['−$40', '−$12', '−$28', '+$12'], a: 2, why: 'Net income impact = −D×(1−t) = −40×0.7 = −$28.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Using the same $40 depreciation charge at a 30% tax rate, what is the CASH FLOW impact?', options: ['−$40', '+$12', '−$28', '$0'], a: 1, why: 'Cash flow impact = the tax shield only = D×t = 40×0.3 = +$12 (a cash benefit from the lower tax bill).' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why does a company prefer MORE depreciation deductions (all else equal), from a pure cash perspective?', options: ['It has no cash effect either way', 'More depreciation lowers taxable income, which lowers the actual cash taxes paid — a genuine cash benefit via the tax shield', 'It increases reported net income', 'It has nothing to do with taxes'], a: 1, why: 'Even though higher depreciation lowers reported net income, its tax-shield effect is a real cash inflow (relative to lower depreciation), which is why accelerated depreciation methods are often used for tax purposes.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Net PP&E starts the year at $300. The company spends $40 on capex and records $25 of depreciation. What is ending net PP&E, and which of those two numbers is a genuinely NEW cash outflow this year?', options: ['$315 ending; the $25 depreciation is the new cash outflow', '$315 ending; the $40 capex is the new cash outflow', '$265 ending; neither is a cash outflow', '$340 ending; both are cash outflows of equal size'], a: 1, why: 'Ending PP&E = 300+40−25 = $315. Capex is a fresh cash outflow (investing activity) this year; depreciation merely allocates a PAST cash outflow and creates no new direct cash movement.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-B =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-B', topic: 'DCF Valuation', title: 'Discounted cash flow: WACC, terminal value, and what the number means', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibb1', name: 'The discount rate: why future cash is worth less, and how WACC blends the cost of two different funding sources',
          primer: 'A discounted cash flow (DCF) valuation is built on one core idea: a dollar promised to you a year from now is worth LESS than a dollar in your hand today, because (a) you could invest today\'s dollar and have MORE than a dollar by next year, and (b) a future promise carries risk that it might not actually arrive. "Discounting" is the mathematical process of converting a future cash amount into its equivalent value today, by dividing it by (1 + discount rate) raised to the power of how many years away it is — the discount rate itself represents the annual return an investor could reasonably expect from an alternative investment of similar risk, so a HIGHER discount rate means future cash is treated as comparatively less valuable today (because better alternatives exist, or because the cash flow is riskier), while a LOWER discount rate means the future cash is barely discounted at all. For an entire company, the relevant discount rate is called the "Weighted Average Cost of Capital" (WACC), and the word "weighted" is the key to understanding it: a company is financed by a MIX of two fundamentally different sources of capital — equity (money from shareholders, who bear the most risk and therefore demand the highest expected return, called the "cost of equity") and debt (money from lenders, who take less risk because they are paid before shareholders and therefore demand a lower return, called the "cost of debt") — and WACC blends these two costs together in proportion to how much of the company\'s total capital comes from each source. One crucial wrinkle: interest paid on debt is tax-deductible (it reduces taxable income, just like depreciation does), so the EFFECTIVE cost of debt to the company is lower than the rate lenders actually charge, once this tax shield is accounted for — this is why WACC always uses the AFTER-TAX cost of debt, multiplying the stated interest rate by (1 minus the tax rate) before blending it in. A HIGHER WACC means the market (or the analyst) is treating the company\'s future cash flows as riskier or less certain, which mechanically produces a LOWER present value / valuation for the exact same projected cash flows — WACC and valuation move in opposite directions.',
          core: 'WACC = (equity weight × cost of equity) + (debt weight × after-tax cost of debt), where after-tax cost of debt = pre-tax cost × (1 − tax rate). A higher WACC discounts future cash flows more harshly, producing a LOWER valuation for the same projected cash flows; a lower WACC produces a HIGHER valuation.',
          when: 'Any DCF valuation question, or any question asking you to blend the cost of equity and debt into a single company-wide discount rate.',
          formulas: ['WACC = (E/V)×Re + (D/V)×Rd×(1−t), where V = E+D (total capital), Re = cost of equity, Rd = pre-tax cost of debt, t = tax rate.', 'Cost of equity (CAPM) = risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium.', 'Discounting a single future cash flow C received n years from now to its present value: PV = C / (1+WACC)ⁿ.'],
          intuition: 'Think of WACC as the "hurdle rate" a company\'s investments must clear to be worth doing at all — if a project is expected to return less than WACC, it destroys value for the people who supplied the capital, even if it looks "profitable" in a simple accounting sense; DCF valuation applies that same hurdle-rate logic to discount the whole company\'s future cash flows back to a present-day price tag.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company is financed 100% by equity with a cost of equity of 10%. What is its WACC? Since there is no debt at all, WACC simply equals the cost of equity: 10%. This confirms the formula collapses sensibly to a single term when one funding source is absent.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A company is financed 70% equity (cost of equity 12%) and 30% debt (pre-tax cost of debt 6%, tax rate 25%). First, after-tax cost of debt = 6%×(1−25%) = 4.5%. WACC = 70%×12% + 30%×4.5% = 8.4% + 1.35% = 9.75%. Notice the after-tax cost of debt (4.5%) is meaningfully cheaper than its pre-tax rate (6%) — this is the tax shield at work, and it is why debt is often called "cheaper" capital than equity, even before considering the raw rate difference.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Two otherwise-identical companies project the exact same $100 cash flow, 5 years from now. Company A has a WACC of 8%; Company B, seen as riskier, has a WACC of 14%. PV for A = 100/(1.08)^5 = 100/1.469 ≈ $68.06. PV for B = 100/(1.14)^5 = 100/1.925 ≈ $51.94. The identical future cash flow is worth over $16 LESS today for the riskier company — this is the direct, quantified consequence of "higher WACC ⇒ lower valuation," and it is exactly why a company\'s perceived riskiness (which raises its cost of equity and/or debt) directly shrinks its DCF-implied value, independent of how good its actual cash flow projections are.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Using the PRE-TAX cost of debt directly in the WACC formula, skipping the after-tax adjustment — this systematically overstates WACC (making debt look more expensive than it truly is to the company) and therefore understates the resulting valuation. Also, confusing "cost of debt" (what lenders demand) with "cost of equity" (what shareholders demand) — these are compensating for very different levels of risk and are never interchangeable.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Why is a dollar received one year from now worth LESS than a dollar today?', options: ['Inflation always destroys exactly one dollar of value per year', 'Because that future dollar could instead have been invested today to grow, and because a future promise carries risk it might not arrive', 'It is not actually worth less — this is a myth', 'Because dollars physically degrade over time'], a: 1, why: 'Both the opportunity cost of not investing it today, and the risk that the future payment does not materialise, justify discounting future cash flows.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'A company is financed 100% by debt, with a pre-tax cost of debt of 8% and a tax rate of 20%. What is its WACC?', options: ['8%', '6.4%', '1.6%', '10%'], a: 1, why: 'With no equity at all, WACC = 100%×after-tax cost of debt = 8%×(1−20%) = 6.4%.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Why does WACC use the AFTER-TAX cost of debt rather than the rate lenders actually charge?', options: ['Because interest is not really paid in cash', 'Because interest expense is tax-deductible, lowering the company\'s actual cash cost of borrowing below the stated interest rate', 'Because equity is always tax-deductible too', 'There is no difference between pre-tax and after-tax cost of debt'], a: 1, why: 'The interest tax shield genuinely lowers the net cost of debt to the company, and WACC is meant to reflect the TRUE blended cost of capital.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A company is 60% equity (cost of equity 11%) and 40% debt (after-tax cost of debt 5%). What is WACC?', options: ['8%', '8.6%', '9.2%', '7.4%'], a: 1, why: '60%×11% + 40%×5% = 6.6% + 2.0% = 8.6%.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'All else equal, if a company\'s perceived risk rises (pushing its cost of equity higher), what happens to its DCF-implied valuation?', options: ['Valuation rises, since risk means higher potential reward', 'Valuation falls, since a higher discount rate shrinks the present value of the same future cash flows', 'No effect — WACC does not affect valuation, only cash flows do', 'It depends only on the tax rate'], a: 1, why: 'A higher cost of equity raises WACC, and a higher WACC discounts every future cash flow more harshly, mechanically lowering the DCF valuation for identical projected cash flows.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Two projects both promise $200 in 3 years. Project X is discounted at 6%, Project Y at 15%. Roughly how much LESS is Project Y worth today, versus Project X?', options: ['They are worth the same today', 'Project Y is worth more today', 'Project Y is worth meaningfully less today — roughly $168 vs $131, a difference of about $37', 'The difference cannot be estimated without more information'], a: 2, why: '200/(1.06)³ ≈ $168; 200/(1.15)³ ≈ $131 — a real, sizeable gap purely from the discount rate, illustrating how much a company\'s perceived risk can move its valuation.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibb2', name: 'Terminal value: valuing the cash flows you cannot possibly project one by one, forever',
          primer: 'A DCF model explicitly projects a company\'s cash flows for only a handful of years into the future — typically 5 to 10 — because trying to forecast the EXACT cash flow of year 47 with any real precision is obviously hopeless; nobody can predict that far ahead with confidence. But a healthy company is not expected to simply STOP existing (and stop generating cash) the moment the explicit projection period ends — so a DCF needs some way to capture the value of ALL the cash flows that will arrive after the projection window closes, lumped together into a single number called the "terminal value," calculated as of the END of the final explicitly projected year. There are two standard, widely accepted methods for estimating terminal value, and it is worth understanding both, because they approach the same problem from genuinely different angles. The "Gordon growth" (or "perpetuity growth") method treats everything after the projection period as an infinite, ever-growing stream of cash flow growing at some modest, sustainable long-run rate FOREVER (typically close to long-run GDP growth, since no company can outgrow the entire economy forever) — it uses the perpetuity formula from basic finance, TV = FCF_final×(1+g) / (WACC−g), which requires growing the final explicit year\'s cash flow by one more year of growth before dividing by the gap between WACC and that growth rate. The "exit multiple" method instead assumes that at the end of the projection period, the company could simply be SOLD to another buyer at a valuation multiple (like EV/EBITDA) similar to what comparable companies trade at TODAY — it is really a comps valuation, just applied at a future point in time rather than today, computed as TV = final-year EBITDA × exit multiple. Whichever method produces the terminal value, that terminal value is itself just a large lump sum sitting at the end of the projection period, so it must ALSO be discounted back to today\'s present value using the exact same WACC-based discounting as every other projected cash flow, using the SAME number of years as the final explicit cash flow.',
          core: 'Terminal value captures everything beyond the explicit projection period, valued as of the end of the final projected year. Gordon growth method: TV = FCF_final×(1+g)/(WACC−g), assuming perpetual modest growth. Exit multiple method: TV = final-year EBITDA × an assumed future trading multiple. Either way, the terminal value must then be discounted back to today just like any other cash flow.',
          when: 'Any DCF question involving cash flows beyond the explicit projection period, or any "how do you value everything after year N" question.',
          formulas: ['Gordon growth terminal value = FCF_final × (1+g) / (WACC − g), where g is the assumed perpetual long-run growth rate (must be strictly less than WACC).', 'Exit multiple terminal value = final projected year EBITDA × assumed exit EV/EBITDA multiple.', 'Present value of the terminal value = TV / (1+WACC)ⁿ, where n = the number of years from today to the end of the final projected year (the same year the TV is computed as of).'],
          intuition: 'It is a striking and important fact in real DCF models that the terminal value often makes up the MAJORITY of a company\'s total implied enterprise value — sometimes 60-80% of it — precisely because it is meant to capture "everything from year 6 or 11 onward, forever." This is exactly why terminal value assumptions (the long-run growth rate, or the assumed future exit multiple) deserve as much scrutiny as the explicit-period forecasts, if not more.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'Final projected year FCF is $50m, long-run growth is 2%, WACC is 9%. Gordon growth TV = 50×(1.02)/(0.09−0.02) = 51/0.07 ≈ $728.6m. Notice how a modest-looking 2% growth rate, divided into a modest-looking 7% gap, produces a terminal value nearly 15× the final year\'s cash flow — perpetuity math compounds this way precisely because "forever" is a very long time.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Same final year, but using the exit multiple method instead: final year EBITDA is $90m, and comparable companies trade at 9× EV/EBITDA. Exit multiple TV = 90×9 = $810m — notice this is a different, independently-derived number from the Gordon growth answer above; the two methods will rarely match exactly, and getting a broadly similar answer from both is often used as a sanity check on the assumptions.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A terminal value of $800m is computed as of the end of year 5. WACC is 10%. What is its value today? PV = 800/(1.10)^5 = 800/1.611 ≈ $496.7m. Notice that even though $800m looks like a large number, discounting it back 5 years at a meaningful WACC shrinks it by nearly 40% — this is exactly why the CHOICE of WACC has such an outsized effect on a DCF\'s final answer: it discounts not just the explicit cash flows, but this large terminal value too.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Forgetting to grow the final projected year\'s cash flow by one more year of growth (the "×(1+g)" term) before applying the Gordon growth formula — this is a very common off-by-one-year error that understates the terminal value. A second, equally common trap: discounting the terminal value back by the WRONG number of years — it must be discounted by the same number of years as the FINAL explicit cash flow, since that is when it is calculated as of, not one year more or less.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Why does a DCF need a "terminal value" at all?', options: ['Because companies always go bankrupt after the projection period', 'Because it is impractical to explicitly project cash flows individually forever, so everything beyond the projection window is captured in one lump-sum estimate', 'Because terminal value is required by accounting law', 'It is optional and rarely used in practice'], a: 1, why: 'Explicit forecasts are only credible for a handful of years; terminal value captures the remaining, much longer future in a single estimate.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'The Gordon growth method assumes cash flows grow:', options: ['At a wildly different rate every year, forever', 'At one modest, constant rate, forever', 'Only for the explicit projection period, then stop entirely', 'At the same rate as inflation only'], a: 1, why: 'The perpetuity growth formula specifically assumes a single constant long-run growth rate continuing indefinitely.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Final year FCF = $40m, growth = 2.5%, WACC = 10%. What is the Gordon growth terminal value (nearest $m)?', options: ['$533m', '$547m', '$400m', '$500m'], a: 1, why: 'TV = 40×1.025/(0.10−0.025) = 41/0.075 ≈ $546.7m ≈ $547m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Final year EBITDA = $70m, exit multiple = 8×. What is the exit-multiple terminal value?', options: ['$560m', '$78m', '$8.75m', '$700m'], a: 0, why: 'TV = 70×8 = $560m.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A terminal value of $600m is computed as of the end of year 6. WACC is 9%. What is its present value today (nearest $m)?', options: ['$550m', '$358m', '$471m', '$600m'], a: 1, why: 'PV = 600/(1.09)^6 = 600/1.677 ≈ $357.8m ≈ $358m — discount by exactly 6 years, matching when the TV is computed as of.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why does terminal value often make up the majority of a DCF\'s total implied value?', options: ['It never actually makes up more than a small fraction', 'Because it represents an infinite (or very long) stream of future cash flows collapsed into one number, and perpetuity math compounds substantially over "forever"', 'Because analysts always deliberately inflate it', 'Because explicit-period cash flows are always negative'], a: 1, why: 'Capturing "everything from year N+1 onward, forever" in one number naturally produces a large figure relative to just a handful of explicit years, especially when growth is close to the discount rate.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-C =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-C', topic: 'Comps & Precedents', title: 'Relative valuation: comparable companies and precedent transactions', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibc1', name: 'Trading comps: valuing a company by what similar companies are worth right now',
          primer: 'A DCF values a company from first principles, by projecting its own cash flows and discounting them — but this is slow, and every assumption (growth, margins, WACC) is a judgment call an analyst could get wrong. "Comparable companies analysis" (almost always shortened to "comps" or "trading comps") takes a completely different, faster approach: find a handful of OTHER publicly-traded companies that are similar to the target in industry, size, growth profile and risk, look at what MULTIPLE of some financial metric (most commonly EV/EBITDA, but also EV/Revenue or P/E) those comparable companies currently trade at in the market, and apply that same multiple to the target company\'s own metric to estimate what the target should be worth. The entire method rests on a simple, powerful assumption: similar businesses, facing similar growth prospects and risks, should trade at broadly similar valuation multiples — if a target looks meaningfully cheaper or more expensive than its true peers on this basis, that difference itself becomes an interesting question (is the target genuinely undervalued, or is there a real reason — worse growth, more risk, weaker margins — that justifies trading at a discount?). Because real-world peer sets always include some companies that are unusually cheap or unusually expensive for reasons that have nothing to do with the target (a one-off event, an accounting quirk, a temporary market overreaction), analysts almost always use the MEDIAN multiple across the peer group rather than the simple average (mean) — the median is far more resistant to being dragged around by one or two extreme outlier companies, exactly the same statistical reason the median is preferred over the mean when summarising incomes or house prices in the real world. Once the appropriate multiple (usually the peer median) is selected, applying it is simple arithmetic: implied Enterprise Value = chosen multiple × the target\'s own value of that same metric, which can then be converted into an implied equity value and share price using the same EV-to-equity bridge covered elsewhere in this track.',
          core: 'Trading comps value a company by applying a peer group\'s valuation multiple (usually the MEDIAN EV/EBITDA, to resist distortion from outliers) to the target\'s own metric: implied EV = peer median multiple × target\'s metric.',
          when: 'Any "value this company using comparable companies / trading multiples" question, or any question asking you to summarise a set of peer multiples.',
          formulas: ['Implied EV = peer median multiple × target\'s own metric (e.g. EBITDA).', 'Median of an odd-sized sorted list = the single middle value; median of an even-sized sorted list = the average of the two middle values.', 'Once implied EV is found, convert to equity value and share price using the standard EV-to-equity bridge (subtract net debt, preferred, minority interest; divide by diluted shares).'],
          intuition: 'Comps analysis is really asking the market itself to do the valuation work for you: instead of building your own opinion of the "right" discount rate and growth assumptions from scratch, you borrow the collective judgment already embedded in how investors are currently pricing similar businesses.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'Five peers trade at EV/EBITDA multiples of 6×, 7×, 8×, 9×, and 20× (the last one is a clear outlier, perhaps due to a recent takeover rumour inflating its price). The simple average is (6+7+8+9+20)/5 = 10.0×, badly distorted by the outlier. The median (sort: 6,7,8,9,20 — middle value) is 8×, a far more representative "typical" multiple for the peer group, unaffected by the one extreme value.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Using the median multiple of 8× from above, and a target with $50m of EBITDA: implied EV = 8× × $50m = $400m.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'The same target has $60m of net debt and 20m diluted shares. What is the implied share price? Equity value = EV − net debt = 400 − 60 = $340m. Share price = 340/20 = $17.00. This shows the full comps-to-price workflow: pick the multiple (median), apply it to get EV, then bridge from EV down to a per-share figure — comps analysis on its own only gets you to enterprise value; the EV-to-equity bridge is a separate, necessary second step.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Using the simple average (mean) of peer multiples instead of the median — a single outlier peer (due to a takeover rumour, a one-off accounting item, or simply being a much larger or smaller company than the others) can distort the mean substantially, while the median stays anchored to the "typical" company in the set.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Comps analysis values a company by:', options: ['Projecting its own cash flows and discounting them', 'Applying a peer group\'s trading multiple to the target\'s own metric', 'Only using its historical stock price', 'Averaging its assets and liabilities'], a: 1, why: 'Comps is a RELATIVE valuation method, benchmarking the target against what similar public companies currently trade at.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why is the median usually preferred over the mean (simple average) when summarising a set of peer multiples?', options: ['The median is always a larger number', 'The median is much less distorted by one or two extreme outlier peers', 'The mean is illegal to use in finance', 'They always give the same answer anyway'], a: 1, why: 'A single unusually cheap or expensive peer can drag the mean far from what is "typical," while the median stays close to the middle of the pack.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Peers trade at 5×, 6×, 6×, 9×, 14×. What is the median?', options: ['8×', '6×', '9×', '6.67×'], a: 1, why: 'Already sorted; the middle (3rd of 5) value is 6×.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Using an 8× median multiple and a target with $35m EBITDA, what is the implied EV?', options: ['$280m', '$43m', '$4.375m', '$35m'], a: 0, why: 'Implied EV = 8× × $35m = $280m.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A comps analysis produces an implied EV of $500m. To get to an implied SHARE PRICE, what must you still do?', options: ['Nothing further — EV and share price are the same thing', 'Subtract net debt (and preferred/minority interest) to get equity value, then divide by diluted shares', 'Multiply by the tax rate', 'Divide by EBITDA again'], a: 1, why: 'Comps only produces enterprise value directly; converting to a per-share figure always requires the separate EV-to-equity bridge.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A peer set has 6 companies (even number) trading at 5×, 6×, 7×, 9×, 10×, 15×. What is the median?', options: ['8×', '7×', '9×', '8.67×'], a: 0, why: 'With an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values (3rd and 4th): (7+9)/2 = 8×.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-D =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-D', topic: 'M&A Mechanics', title: 'Merger models: why the market rewards or punishes a deal', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibd1', name: 'Accretion and dilution: what it means, and why the market does not just reward "bigger"', track: 'ib',
          primer: 'When one company (the "acquirer") buys another (the "target"), the immediate, mechanical question a merger model answers is: does the acquirer\'s earnings per share (EPS) go UP or DOWN as a direct result of the deal? If combined pro-forma EPS after the deal is HIGHER than the acquirer\'s standalone EPS before the deal, the deal is called "accretive." If pro-forma EPS is LOWER, the deal is "dilutive." The crucial, easy-to-miss point is that this is a comparison of PER-SHARE earnings, not total earnings — a deal can straightforwardly increase the acquirer\'s total combined net income (after all, you are simply adding the target\'s profit to your own) while STILL being dilutive on a per-share basis, if the number of new shares issued to fund the deal grows FASTER than the combined earnings do. This matters enormously in practice because the stock market reacts very strongly, very quickly, to whether a newly announced deal appears accretive or dilutive — an accretive deal is generally (though not always, and not automatically) seen as a signal that the acquirer got the target for a "cheap enough" price relative to its own valuation, while a dilutive deal raises an immediate question: why is management willing to shrink earnings per existing shareholder\'s share? Whether a deal ends up accretive or dilutive depends heavily on HOW it is financed. An all-CASH deal (funded from the acquirer\'s existing cash balance) issues no new shares at all, so the share count does not grow — the deal is accretive as long as the target\'s earnings yield (net income / purchase price) exceeds the after-tax opportunity cost of the cash used (the return that cash was otherwise earning). An all-STOCK deal issues new acquirer shares to pay the target\'s shareholders, diluting the share count directly, so accretion depends on whether the target\'s earnings yield exceeds the acquirer\'s own earnings yield (a rough rule of thumb: buying a company at a LOWER P/E than your own tends to be accretive; buying at a HIGHER P/E than your own tends to be dilutive). Crucially, a dilutive deal is not automatically a BAD deal — a management team might rationally accept short-term EPS dilution if the strategic logic is strong enough: entering a valuable new market, eliminating a dangerous competitor, acquiring technology or talent that could not otherwise be built in time, or unlocking cost synergies that will make the deal accretive again within a year or two even though it is dilutive on day one.',
          core: 'A deal is accretive if pro-forma combined EPS exceeds the acquirer\'s standalone EPS, dilutive if it is lower — this is a PER-SHARE comparison, not a total-earnings comparison. Financing matters: cash deals avoid new share issuance; stock deals dilute the share count directly, roughly favouring accretion when the target trades at a lower P/E than the acquirer. Dilutive deals are not automatically bad if the strategic logic (synergies, market access, competitive necessity) justifies the short-term EPS cost.',
          when: 'Any merger-model question asking whether a deal is accretive or dilutive, or any question about why the market reacts positively or negatively to an M&A announcement.',
          formulas: ['Accretion/dilution % = (Pro-forma EPS / acquirer standalone EPS − 1) × 100.', 'All-stock deal: exchange ratio = deal price per target share / acquirer\'s share price. New shares issued = target\'s share count × exchange ratio.', 'Pro-forma EPS = combined net income (acquirer + target, net of any new financing costs and synergies) / (acquirer\'s original shares + newly issued shares).'],
          intuition: 'Picture EPS as "profit sliced into portions, one portion per existing share" — an accretive deal means each EXISTING slice gets a little bigger after the deal; a dilutive deal means the pie grew, but it also got cut into so many MORE slices that each individual slice actually shrank.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'Acquirer has $100m net income and 50m shares (EPS = $2.00). It buys a target for cash (no new shares issued), adding $20m of net income with no other adjustments. Combined net income = $120m, shares stay at 50m. Pro-forma EPS = 120/50 = $2.40. This is accretive: EPS rose from $2.00 to $2.40, a +20% change — an all-cash deal, with no financing cost assumed, is accretive here simply because it adds profit without adding any new shares.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Same acquirer ($100m NI, 50m shares, EPS $2.00) instead does an all-STOCK deal, issuing 30m new shares to acquire a target that adds $20m of net income. Combined net income = $120m. New share count = 50+30 = 80m. Pro-forma EPS = 120/80 = $1.50. This is DILUTIVE — EPS fell from $2.00 to $1.50, a −25% change — even though total combined net income rose by $20m, because the share count grew by 60% (from 50m to 80m), far outpacing the 20% growth in net income.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Why did the cash deal in the first example accrete while the stock deal in the second example diluted, even though both added the exact same $20m of target net income? The difference is entirely in the denominator: the cash deal added zero new shares, so 100% of the extra $20m flowed straight to existing shareholders\' per-share earnings. The stock deal added 30m new shares (a 60% increase to the share count) to fund the purchase, and that dramatic increase in the number of slices outweighed the increase in the size of the pie. This illustrates the core lesson: financing structure, not just the target\'s profitability, drives whether a deal is accretive or dilutive.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Assuming that because combined net income rose, the deal must be "good" or accretive — always check the PER-SHARE figure, since a large new share issuance can turn a genuinely profit-boosting acquisition into an EPS-dilutive one. The reverse trap also matters: assuming a dilutive deal is automatically a bad decision, when strong-enough strategic rationale (synergies, market access, competitive necessity) can justify short-term dilution.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'A deal is "accretive" when:', options: ['Total combined net income rises', 'Pro-forma EPS is higher than the acquirer\'s standalone EPS', 'The acquirer pays entirely in cash', 'The target\'s revenue is larger than the acquirer\'s'], a: 1, why: 'Accretion/dilution is specifically a per-share comparison, not a total-earnings or deal-structure comparison.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Is it possible for combined net income to rise while EPS still falls?', options: ['No, that is a contradiction', 'Yes — if new shares are issued fast enough, per-share earnings can fall even as total earnings rise', 'Only if the target has negative net income', 'Only in an all-cash deal'], a: 1, why: 'This is exactly the mechanism of dilution in a stock-funded deal: the earnings pie grows, but it is sliced into even more pieces.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Acquirer: $80m net income, 40m shares. All-cash deal adds $15m of net income with no new shares issued. What is the new EPS, and is it accretive or dilutive?', options: ['$2.375, accretive', '$2.00, no change', '$1.90, dilutive', '$2.375, dilutive'], a: 0, why: 'New EPS = (80+15)/40 = $2.375, up from 80/40 = $2.00 — accretive.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'All-stock deal roughly favours accretion when:', options: ['The target trades at a HIGHER P/E than the acquirer', 'The target trades at a LOWER P/E than the acquirer', 'P/E is irrelevant to accretion/dilution', 'The acquirer has more total debt than the target'], a: 1, why: 'Buying earnings "cheaply" (at a lower P/E than your own) in stock tends to be accretive, since you are issuing relatively fewer of your (expensively-valued) shares per dollar of acquired earnings.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A board approves a dilutive stock deal despite the EPS hit. Which is the STRONGEST justification?', options: ['Dilutive deals are always secretly accretive if you wait long enough, with no further explanation needed', 'The deal provides critical technology/market access/cost synergies whose value is not yet reflected in near-term EPS', 'The target\'s CEO insisted on it', 'Dilution never matters to any stakeholder'], a: 1, why: 'Legitimate strategic rationale — synergies, competitive necessity, capability that could not otherwise be built in time — can outweigh a short-term EPS cost; simply asserting deals "become accretive eventually" with no mechanism is not a justification.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Acquirer EPS is $3.00. A stock deal results in pro-forma EPS of $2.70. What is the accretion/dilution percentage?', options: ['+10%', '−10%', '−30%', '+30%'], a: 1, why: '(2.70/3.00 − 1) × 100 = −10% — a 10% dilutive deal.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-E =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-E', topic: 'LBO', title: 'Leveraged buyouts: how borrowed money changes the math of a return', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibe1', name: 'Sources and uses, and why leverage is the whole point of an LBO',
          primer: 'A leveraged buyout ("LBO") is the purchase of a company financed with a large amount of borrowed money (debt) alongside a smaller slice of the buyer\'s own cash (equity) — the word "leveraged" refers directly to this heavy use of debt, or "leverage," to fund the purchase. The private equity firm doing the buying is usually called the "sponsor." Every LBO can be summarised with a simple "sources and uses" table: the "uses" side lists everything the money is needed FOR (overwhelmingly, this is the purchase price of the company itself, often called the purchase enterprise value, plus transaction fees); the "sources" side lists everywhere the money comes FROM to cover those uses (a large slice of new debt raised against the target company, plus the sponsor\'s own equity check, which mathematically must be whatever is left over once debt is subtracted — sources and uses must always balance exactly, by definition). Why go to the trouble of borrowing so much money, rather than simply paying all-cash from the sponsor\'s own funds? Because leverage dramatically amplifies the RETURN on the sponsor\'s own invested capital, for a given amount of value growth — this is the single most important intuition in all of LBO analysis. If a company is bought entirely with the sponsor\'s own cash and later sold for a higher price, the sponsor\'s percentage return simply equals the percentage growth in the company\'s value. But if the same company is bought using mostly borrowed money, the SAME dollar amount of value growth accrues almost entirely to the much SMALLER equity check the sponsor actually put in — the lenders who provided the debt do not get any extra upside if the company does well (they were promised a fixed return, not a share of the growth), so all of the "excess" appreciation flows disproportionately to the equity holder. This amplification effect cuts both ways, however: leverage also magnifies LOSSES in the same proportion, which is exactly why heavily-levered companies are considered financially riskier — a modest decline in company value can wipe out a highly-levered equity position entirely, even while the same decline would only modestly dent an all-equity-funded purchase.',
          core: 'Sources (debt raised + sponsor equity) must equal uses (purchase price + fees). Using a large proportion of debt shrinks the sponsor\'s own equity check for the same purchase price, so a given amount of dollar value growth translates into a much larger PERCENTAGE return on that smaller equity base — leverage amplifies both gains and losses.',
          when: 'Any LBO question about how a deal is financed, or any question asking WHY leverage boosts (or endangers) private equity returns.',
          formulas: ['Uses: Purchase enterprise value = entry EV/EBITDA multiple × EBITDA (plus any transaction fees).', 'Sources: Debt = leverage multiple × EBITDA (a separate multiple from the entry purchase multiple). Sponsor equity = total uses − debt raised (the "plug" that balances the table).', 'Leverage amplification (intuition check): a smaller equity base means the SAME dollar gain in company value produces a LARGER percentage return on that equity.'],
          intuition: 'Think of buying a $1,000,000 house with a $900,000 mortgage and only $100,000 of your own cash: if the house rises in value by $100,000 (10% of its price), your own $100,000 investment has DOUBLED — a 100% return — even though the underlying asset only grew 10%. LBOs apply exactly this same mortgage-style amplification to buying companies instead of houses.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A sponsor buys a company for 8× its $50m EBITDA ($400m purchase price), financed with debt of 5× EBITDA ($250m). Sponsor equity (the plug) = 400 − 250 = $150m.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Suppose that company is sold 5 years later for exactly the same $400m enterprise value (no EBITDA growth, no multiple change), but $100m of the original $250m debt has been paid down using the company\'s free cash flow over those 5 years, leaving $150m of debt outstanding. Exit equity value = exit EV − remaining debt = 400 − 150 = $250m. MOIC (multiple of invested capital) = exit equity / entry equity = 250/150 ≈ 1.67×. Notice the underlying business did not grow in value at ALL (still worth $400m) — the entire 1.67× return came purely from paying down debt with the company\'s own cash flow, converting debt into equity value over time. This is the "deleveraging" return driver, distinct from business growth.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Compare that same $150m of value growth (from $400m purchase EV rising, hypothetically, to $550m at exit with debt fully repaid, i.e. exit equity = $550m) under two different financing structures: (a) an all-cash purchase (equity = full $400m, no debt): return = 550/400 = 1.375× MOIC. (b) The levered purchase from above (equity = $150m): if the same $150m of value growth accrued and debt was fully repaid, exit equity = 550−0 = $550m, MOIC = 550/150 ≈ 3.67×. The identical $150m of dollar-value growth produces a 37.5% return in the unlevered case but a return of nearly 267% in the levered case — this stark contrast is the entire reason leverage is central to how private equity generates outsized returns.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Comparing exit EQUITY value to entry ENTERPRISE value (or vice versa) when computing a return — MOIC must always compare equity-to-equity, since the sponsor only owns the equity slice, not the whole enterprise value (debt holders have their own separate, fixed claim and do not share in the equity upside).',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'In "sources and uses," what must always be true?', options: ['Sources exceed uses by the amount of fees', 'Sources and uses must exactly balance', 'Uses always exceed sources', 'There is no required relationship between them'], a: 1, why: 'Every dollar of "use" (what the money is needed for) must be funded by exactly one dollar of "source" (where the money comes from) — they balance by definition.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why do private equity sponsors use a large amount of debt rather than paying all-cash?', options: ['Debt is required by law for all acquisitions', 'Leverage amplifies the percentage return on the sponsor\'s smaller equity check, for the same dollar amount of value growth', 'It has no effect on returns at all', 'Debt eliminates all financial risk'], a: 1, why: 'A smaller equity base means the same dollar gain in enterprise value translates into a much larger percentage return on that equity — the central mechanic of leveraged returns.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Purchase price is 7× a $60m EBITDA, financed with debt of 4× EBITDA. What is the required sponsor equity?', options: ['$420m', '$240m', '$180m', '$60m'], a: 2, why: 'Purchase EV = 7×60 = $420m. Debt = 4×60 = $240m. Sponsor equity = 420−240 = $180m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Entry equity was $150m; exit equity value is $300m. What is the MOIC?', options: ['1.5×', '2.0×', '0.5×', '3.0×'], a: 1, why: 'MOIC = exit equity / entry equity = 300/150 = 2.0×.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A company\'s enterprise value does not change at all over the hold period, yet the sponsor still earns a positive MOIC above 1.0×. What is the most likely explanation?', options: ['This is impossible — MOIC must equal 1.0× if EV is unchanged', 'Debt was paid down using the company\'s free cash flow during the hold, converting debt into equity value even with flat EV', 'The exit multiple must secretly have expanded', 'EBITDA must have grown'], a: 1, why: 'Deleveraging (paying down debt with the company\'s own cash flow) is itself a distinct source of equity value growth, entirely separate from EBITDA growth or multiple expansion — flat enterprise value with debt paydown still produces a return above 1.0×.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why is comparing exit EQUITY value to entry ENTERPRISE value (instead of entry equity) the wrong way to compute a sponsor\'s return?', options: ['It actually is the correct method', 'The sponsor only invested the entry EQUITY check (not the whole enterprise value, most of which was debt), so the return must be measured against that actual equity investment', 'Enterprise value and equity value are always identical', 'Debt holders share equally in the sponsor\'s return'], a: 1, why: 'MOIC measures the return on what the sponsor actually put IN (the equity check), not the total transaction size, most of which was funded by lenders with their own separate, fixed claim.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-F =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-F', topic: 'M&A Mechanics', title: 'Purchase accounting: goodwill, and counting shares the right way', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibf1', name: 'Goodwill: the plug that appears when an acquirer pays more than book value',
          primer: 'Every asset on a company\'s balance sheet has a "book value" — the amount it is recorded at, generally based on what the company originally paid for it (its historical cost), possibly reduced over time by depreciation or amortisation. "Fair value," by contrast, is what an asset would actually sell for today in an arm\'s-length transaction between a willing buyer and a willing seller — and fair value is very often DIFFERENT from book value, sometimes dramatically so, especially for assets a company built up internally over many years (a strong brand, loyal customers, valuable trade secrets) that accounting rules never let the company record on its own balance sheet in the first place, precisely because they were never purchased in an identifiable transaction with a clear price tag. When one company (the acquirer) buys another company (the target) in an M&A deal, accounting rules require the acquirer to first identify and revalue every one of the target\'s individual assets and liabilities to FAIR VALUE as of the acquisition date — these revalued items are called "net identifiable assets" (identifiable meaning each one can be separately named, valued and, in principle, sold on its own — a factory, a patent, a customer contract). Almost always, the total purchase price the acquirer actually pays is HIGHER than this fair value of net identifiable assets — because the acquirer is not just buying a pile of separately identifiable items, it is buying the whole, functioning BUSINESS, including things that cannot be individually identified and valued on their own: an established brand reputation, a trained and assembled workforce, customer relationships built over years, and the expected SYNERGIES the acquirer believes it can unlock by combining the two businesses. "Goodwill" is simply the accounting plug that captures this entire remaining difference: Goodwill = Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets. It sits on the acquirer\'s balance sheet as a long-term asset, but it is fundamentally unlike a physical asset such as a factory or a truck — you cannot sell goodwill on its own, touch it, or use it directly to produce anything — yet it genuinely represents real, paid-for economic value, since the acquirer was willing to pay real cash (or stock) for exactly the intangible qualities goodwill is capturing.',
          core: 'Goodwill = Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets. It captures everything the acquirer paid for beyond the target\'s individually identifiable assets and liabilities — brand, workforce, customer relationships, expected synergies — and sits on the balance sheet as a long-term asset even though it has no independent physical existence.',
          when: 'Any M&A purchase-price-allocation question, or any question about why an acquirer\'s balance sheet shows a large intangible asset after a deal closes.',
          formulas: ['Goodwill = Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets, where Net Identifiable Assets = (fair value of identifiable assets acquired) − (fair value of liabilities assumed).', 'Total consideration paid = cash + stock issued + assumed debt (if applicable) − any cash already on the target\'s balance sheet, depending on deal structure.'],
          intuition: 'Think of goodwill as the price tag on everything a seller built that never showed up on their own balance sheet — a company can spend decades building a trusted brand and loyal customers, but accounting rules only let a BUYER record that value, and only at the moment of an actual purchase, which is exactly why goodwill only ever appears as a direct RESULT of an acquisition, never organically.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'An acquirer pays $500m for a target. A fair-value appraisal finds the target\'s identifiable assets are worth $420m and its liabilities are $70m. Net identifiable assets = 420−70 = $350m. Goodwill = 500−350 = $150m.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'The same target\'s BOOK value (per its own pre-deal balance sheet) showed assets of $380m and liabilities of $70m, for book net assets of $310m — different from the $350m fair value used above, because several assets (like an internally-developed patent portfolio, never capitalised at its true worth under the target\'s own accounting) were revalued upward during the acquisition\'s fair-value appraisal. This illustrates that goodwill is computed against FAIR value of net identifiable assets, not the target\'s own pre-deal BOOK value — using book value instead would overstate goodwill by exactly the amount of that revaluation ($350m−$310m = $40m too much).' },
            { level: 3, text: 'One year after the deal, the acquired business performs far worse than expected — a key customer relationship (part of what was implicitly paid for via goodwill) is lost, and the expected synergies never materialise. The acquirer\'s accountants determine the acquired business is now only worth $100m less than what goodwill implied. This triggers a "goodwill impairment": a $100m non-cash charge is recorded on the income statement, reducing net income by $100m (with a possible partial tax offset depending on jurisdiction) and reducing the goodwill asset on the balance sheet by the same $100m — the ORIGINAL cash was already spent at the time of acquisition, so, just like depreciation, no NEW cash actually leaves the company at the moment the impairment is recorded; it purely reflects a re-assessment that the price paid can no longer be justified by the business\'s value.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Computing goodwill against the target\'s own pre-deal BOOK value of net assets, instead of the FAIR value determined during the acquisition\'s purchase-price allocation — book value and fair value are frequently different, and using the wrong one produces the wrong goodwill figure.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Goodwill is calculated as:', options: ['Purchase price minus the target\'s book value of equity', 'Purchase price minus the fair value of net identifiable assets acquired', 'The target\'s brand value alone', 'Purchase price plus transaction fees'], a: 1, why: 'Goodwill is specifically the excess of purchase price over the FAIR value of net identifiable assets, not book value.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why can an acquirer not simply buy "goodwill" on its own, the way it could buy a factory?', options: ['Goodwill is illegal to purchase directly', 'Goodwill is not an individually identifiable asset — it represents the combined, unseparated value of brand, workforce, relationships and expected synergies from the WHOLE acquired business', 'Goodwill is the same thing as cash', 'Only public companies can have goodwill'], a: 1, why: 'Goodwill is defined as exactly the leftover value that cannot be attributed to any single identifiable asset.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'An acquirer pays $800m for a target with fair-value identifiable assets of $650m and liabilities of $100m. What is goodwill?', options: ['$150m', '$250m', '$800m', '$50m'], a: 1, why: 'Net identifiable assets = 650−100 = $550m. Goodwill = 800−550 = $250m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Is goodwill a "real" asset in the same physical sense as a factory or a piece of machinery?', options: ['Yes, it is identical in every respect', 'No — it has no independent physical existence and cannot be sold on its own, yet it still represents genuine, paid-for economic value', 'No, and it has no economic value at all', 'Goodwill is simply an accounting error'], a: 1, why: 'Goodwill captures real economic value (brand, relationships, synergies) even though it is not a physical, separately-sellable asset.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A goodwill impairment charge of $60m is recorded. What is its DIRECT, immediate effect on cash?', options: ['Cash falls by $60m immediately', 'Cash is unaffected — the charge is non-cash, reducing net income and the goodwill asset, but no new cash leaves the company at the moment of impairment', 'Cash rises by $60m', 'Cash falls by $60m, spread evenly over the next 5 years'], a: 1, why: 'Like a depreciation write-down, an impairment is a re-assessment of an ALREADY-spent purchase price — no new cash moves at the moment the impairment is recorded.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A company\'s balance sheet shows a very large goodwill balance built up over many years. What does this most directly suggest about the company\'s history?', options: ['The company has never made an acquisition', 'The company has a history of paying prices above the fair value of acquired companies\' identifiable net assets — i.e. an active M&A/acquisition history', 'The company is definitely overvalued today', 'Goodwill says nothing about a company\'s past'], a: 1, why: 'Since goodwill only arises from acquisitions priced above fair value of net identifiable assets, a large accumulated goodwill balance is a direct signature of a substantial acquisition history.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibf2', name: 'The treasury stock method: getting from basic shares to fully diluted shares',
          primer: 'A company\'s "basic" share count is simply the number of common shares currently outstanding and held by shareholders today. But many companies have also granted stock OPTIONS to employees or executives — a stock option is a contractual right (not an obligation) to buy a share of the company at a fixed price, called the "strike price" (or "exercise price"), at some point in the future. An option is "in-the-money" when the company\'s CURRENT market share price is HIGHER than the option\'s strike price — meaning the option holder could exercise the option (buy at the lower strike price) and immediately have a share worth more than what they paid, a real, valuable right; an option is "out-of-the-money" when the strike price exceeds the current market price, meaning exercising it would mean paying MORE than the share is currently worth, so a rational holder would simply not exercise it. Because in-the-money options represent shares that COULD reasonably be issued (if the holders chose to exercise), any calculation of "fully diluted" shares — used for both diluted EPS and for computing the ACTUAL number of shares an acquirer must pay for in an M&A deal — needs to account for them, and the "treasury stock method" (TSM) is the standard way to do this correctly. The method works in three explicit steps: first, assume every in-the-money option is exercised, which brings in a definite amount of cash to the company (proceeds = number of options × strike price per option) — this cash is REAL money the company receives from the option holders as the price of exercising. Second, assume the company immediately uses that cash to hypothetically buy back its own shares from the open market, at the CURRENT market price — the number of shares this hypothetical buyback can afford is proceeds ÷ current market price. Third, the NET new shares actually added to the share count is the difference between the number of shares issued upon exercise and the number of shares hypothetically repurchased with the proceeds: since the exercise price is below the market price for in-the-money options, the repurchase can only buy back FEWER shares than were issued, so the net effect is always a modest INCREASE in the share count (dilution), never a decrease.',
          core: 'Treasury stock method: (1) in-the-money options are assumed exercised, generating cash proceeds = options × strike price; (2) that cash is assumed used to buy back shares at the CURRENT market price; (3) net new shares = options exercised − shares repurchased. This net figure, added to basic shares, gives fully diluted shares.',
          when: 'Any question computing diluted EPS, fully diluted share count, or the true number of shares an acquirer must pay for in a deal.',
          formulas: ['Cash proceeds from exercise = number of in-the-money options × strike price.', 'Shares hypothetically repurchased = cash proceeds / current market share price.', 'Net new (diluted) shares from options = number of options exercised − shares hypothetically repurchased.', 'Fully diluted shares = basic shares outstanding + net new shares from all in-the-money options/warrants/convertibles.'],
          intuition: 'The treasury stock method assumes the company does not just hand out free shares when options are exercised — it collects real cash for them first, and that cash has real purchasing power to buy back some shares in return, which is exactly why the dilution from in-the-money options is always SMALLER than simply counting every option as a brand-new free share.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: '10 million options are outstanding with a strike price of $20, and the current market price is $30 (in-the-money, since $30 > $20). Step 1: exercise proceeds = 10m × $20 = $200m. Step 2: shares repurchased with that cash = $200m / $30 = 6.67m shares. Step 3: net new shares = 10m − 6.67m = 3.33m.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A company has 200m basic shares outstanding. Using the same options from above (net new shares = 3.33m), fully diluted shares = 200m + 3.33m = 203.33m. Net income of $400m gives basic EPS of 400/200 = $2.00, but diluted EPS of 400/203.33 ≈ $1.967 — slightly lower, since the same profit is now spread across a slightly larger effective share count.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A company has two option tranches: 5m options at a $15 strike (market price $30, deeply in-the-money) and 8m options at a $35 strike (market price $30, OUT-of-the-money, since $35 > $30). Only the first tranche is included in the TSM calculation — the second tranche is ignored entirely, since a rational holder would never exercise an option that costs more than the share is currently worth. Tranche 1: proceeds = 5m×$15 = $75m; shares repurchased = $75m/$30 = 2.5m; net new shares = 5m−2.5m = 2.5m. The 8m out-of-the-money options contribute ZERO dilution under the treasury stock method, regardless of how many of them exist.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Including OUT-of-the-money options in the treasury stock method calculation — only IN-the-money options (strike price below the current market price) are assumed exercised; out-of-the-money options contribute no dilution at all, since exercising them would be irrational.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'An option is "in-the-money" when:', options: ['The strike price is above the current market price', 'The current market price is above the strike price', 'The option has just been granted', 'The company has no cash'], a: 1, why: 'In-the-money means exercising is profitable: the holder buys at the (lower) strike price a share currently worth more on the market.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'What is the FIRST step of the treasury stock method?', options: ['Assume the company buys back shares with existing cash', 'Assume in-the-money options are exercised, generating cash proceeds equal to options × strike price', 'Assume the share price falls to the strike price', 'Ignore all options entirely'], a: 1, why: 'The method starts by assuming exercise happens and cash is collected, before considering any hypothetical buyback.' },
            { level: 2, q: '4 million options have a $10 strike price; the market price is $25. What are the cash proceeds from assumed exercise?', options: ['$100m', '$40m', '$25m', '$15m'], a: 1, why: 'Proceeds = 4m × $10 = $40m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Using the $40m of proceeds from the previous question, and a $25 market price, how many shares can be hypothetically repurchased?', options: ['4 million', '1.6 million', '2.5 million', '10 million'], a: 1, why: '$40m / $25 = 1.6 million shares.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Continuing the example (4m options exercised, 1.6m shares hypothetically repurchased), what is the NET new share count added by these options?', options: ['4 million', '1.6 million', '2.4 million', '5.6 million'], a: 2, why: 'Net new shares = options exercised − shares repurchased = 4m − 1.6m = 2.4m.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A company has 3m options at a $40 strike price, while the current market price is $32. How many net new shares does the treasury stock method add for this tranche?', options: ['3 million', '0.24 million', '2.4 million', 'Zero — these options are out-of-the-money and are excluded entirely'], a: 3, why: 'Since $40 (strike) > $32 (market price), these options are out-of-the-money and are not assumed exercised under the treasury stock method, contributing no dilution.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-G =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-G', topic: 'Accounting Flows', title: 'Working capital as an operating and deal-making lens, and the double edge of fixed costs', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibg1', name: 'Net working capital: an operating efficiency signal, and a battleground in every M&A deal',
          primer: '"Net working capital" (NWC) is defined as Current Assets minus Current Liabilities, where "current" specifically means items expected to convert to cash or come due within about one year. The most important current assets for this purpose are accounts receivable (money owed TO the company by customers who have already been billed but have not yet paid) and inventory (goods purchased or manufactured but not yet sold); the most important current liability is accounts payable (money the company owes TO its own suppliers, for goods or services already received but not yet paid for). NWC matters enormously for free cash flow (FCF) because it represents cash that is genuinely TIED UP in the day-to-day operating cycle of the business rather than being available to the company for other uses: when NWC INCREASES — for instance, because receivables grow faster than payables, meaning the company is extending more credit to customers than it is receiving from suppliers — that increase is a direct CASH OUTFLOW that reduces FCF, dollar for dollar, even though it never appears as an "expense" on the income statement at all. NWC becomes an especially contested topic in M&A deal negotiations: buyers do not want to pay the FULL enterprise value and then discover the seller has stripped the business of its normal operating cash cushion right before closing (for instance, by aggressively collecting all outstanding receivables early, delaying supplier payments to inflate payables, or running inventory down to unusually low levels) — any of these tactics would artificially boost the SELLER\'s cash position at the buyer\'s expense, leaving the buyer with a business that needs an immediate cash injection just to keep operating normally. To prevent this, M&A agreements almost always specify a "target" or "normalized" level of NWC that must be delivered at closing (based on the business\'s recent historical average), with a post-closing "true-up" adjustment to the purchase price if the ACTUAL NWC delivered differs from that target — if the seller delivers LESS NWC than the target, the purchase price is reduced by the shortfall (and vice versa). Three specific ratios are commonly used to monitor NWC efficiency over time: Days Sales Outstanding (DSO, how many days on average it takes to collect on receivables — LOWER is generally better, since cash is collected faster), Days Inventory Outstanding (DIO, how many days inventory sits before being sold — LOWER is generally better, since capital is not tied up as long), and Days Payable Outstanding (DPO, how many days the company takes to pay its own suppliers — HIGHER is generally better for the company\'s own cash position, though pushed too far it can strain supplier relationships).',
          core: 'NWC = Current Assets − Current Liabilities (mainly receivables + inventory − payables). A RISING NWC ties up cash and directly REDUCES free cash flow. In M&A, buyers negotiate a "normalized" NWC target and adjust the purchase price if actual NWC at closing falls short (protecting against a seller artificially stripping cash before close). DSO and DIO measure collection/inventory speed (lower is generally better); DPO measures how slowly the company pays suppliers (higher is generally better for its own cash, within reason).',
          when: 'Any question about how a change in receivables/inventory/payables affects free cash flow, or about how NWC is handled in an M&A purchase agreement.',
          formulas: ['NWC = Current Assets − Current Liabilities ≈ (Receivables + Inventory) − Payables, for operating purposes.', 'Impact on FCF: an INCREASE in NWC is a USE of cash (subtract from FCF); a DECREASE in NWC is a SOURCE of cash (add to FCF).', 'DSO = (Accounts Receivable / Revenue) × 365. DIO = (Inventory / COGS) × 365. DPO = (Accounts Payable / COGS) × 365.', 'M&A purchase price adjustment ≈ (actual NWC delivered at closing − target/normalized NWC), added to or subtracted from the agreed purchase price.'],
          intuition: 'NWC is the cash quietly "parked" inside the operating cycle of the business at any given moment — money already spent on inventory not yet sold, or already earned but not yet collected from customers — and any INCREASE in that parked amount is cash the company genuinely does not have available for anything else, exactly as real a cash outflow as writing a check, even though no single expense line records it.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company\'s receivables rise by $8m and inventory rises by $5m this quarter, while payables rise by $6m. Change in NWC = (8+5) − 6 = +$7m. Since NWC rose, this is a USE of cash, reducing free cash flow by $7m this quarter — even though net income might be perfectly healthy.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A target has average DSO of 45 days, but a review of the last 30 days before a deal\'s scheduled closing shows DSO suddenly dropped to 20 days (much faster than normal collection). This is a red flag for the buyer: it suggests the seller may have aggressively pushed customers to pay early (perhaps by offering steep early-payment discounts) specifically to inflate the cash position handed over at closing, temporarily and artificially improving the NWC figure right before the purchase price is locked in.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'An M&A agreement sets a target NWC of $50m at closing, based on the trailing 12-month average. At the actual closing date, NWC is measured at $44m — $6m BELOW target. What happens to the purchase price? The purchase price is reduced by $6m (the shortfall), since the buyer is effectively receiving $6m less operating cash cushion than was assumed when the original enterprise value was agreed — this "true-up" mechanism is exactly why sellers cannot profitably strip working capital before closing: any shortfall from the target simply comes directly out of the final purchase price they receive.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Forgetting that a RISING net working capital reduces free cash flow (it is a cash USE, not a cash source) — this is easy to get backwards, especially since a "rising" figure often intuitively sounds like a positive sign, when here it specifically means more cash is being tied up in operations.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Net working capital is defined as:', options: ['Total assets minus total liabilities', 'Current assets minus current liabilities', 'Cash minus debt', 'Revenue minus costs'], a: 1, why: 'NWC specifically nets the short-term (current) assets against the short-term (current) liabilities.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'A rise in net working capital has what effect on free cash flow?', options: ['It increases FCF', 'It decreases FCF, since cash is being tied up in operations', 'It has no effect on FCF', 'It only affects the balance sheet, never cash flow'], a: 1, why: 'An increase in NWC is a use of cash, directly reducing FCF for that period.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Receivables rise $10m, inventory falls $4m, payables rise $3m. What is the net change in NWC?', options: ['+$3m', '+$17m', '+$9m', '−$3m'], a: 0, why: 'ΔNWC = (+10 − 4) − 3 = 6 − 3 = +3.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Which ratio measures how many days, on average, it takes a company to collect on its receivables?', options: ['DPO', 'DIO', 'DSO', 'MOIC'], a: 2, why: 'DSO (Days Sales Outstanding) specifically measures collection speed on receivables.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why might a buyer in an M&A deal insist on a "normalized" NWC target with a post-closing purchase price adjustment?', options: ['To make the deal more complicated for no reason', 'To prevent the seller from artificially stripping operating cash out of the business right before closing, leaving the buyer with an under-cushioned business', 'Because NWC has no real economic significance', 'Purchase price adjustments are only used for very small deals'], a: 1, why: 'The target-and-true-up mechanism specifically protects the buyer from a seller manipulating NWC to inflate their proceeds at the buyer\'s expense.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A target\'s DSO drops sharply in the final month before a deal\'s scheduled close, well below its historical average. What should this signal to a careful buyer?', options: ['Nothing — faster collection is always simply good news with no further investigation needed', 'A possible red flag: the seller may be temporarily accelerating collections to inflate the NWC/cash position delivered at closing', 'That the company\'s revenue must be falling', 'That accounts payable must also be falling by the same amount'], a: 1, why: 'A sudden, temporary improvement in a working-capital metric right before a deal closes is a classic signal worth investigating for possible manipulation ahead of the NWC true-up.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibg2', name: 'Operating leverage: how a fixed-cost structure amplifies both good news and bad news',
          primer: 'Every company\'s costs can be sorted into two broad categories. "Fixed costs" are expenses that stay roughly the SAME regardless of how much the company actually produces or sells in a given period — rent on a factory, insurance premiums, and the base salaries of permanent staff are classic examples, since the company owes these amounts whether it sells one unit or one million units. "Variable costs," by contrast, scale up and down directly WITH the volume of production or sales — raw materials, shipping costs, and sales commissions are classic examples, since they genuinely depend on how much is actually produced or sold. "Operating leverage" describes how much of a company\'s total cost base is fixed versus variable, and it has a powerful, somewhat counter-intuitive consequence: a company with HIGH operating leverage (a large proportion of fixed costs relative to variable costs) will see its operating income swing by a much LARGER percentage than its revenue does, in EITHER direction — a modest revenue increase can produce a dramatically larger percentage increase in operating income (since the fixed costs do not grow at all, so almost every extra dollar of revenue drops straight through to profit after covering variable costs), but equally, a modest revenue DECLINE can produce a dramatically larger percentage DECLINE in operating income (since the fixed costs still have to be paid in full, even as revenue shrinks, eating directly into a thinner profit margin). This is precisely why industries with very expensive fixed infrastructure — airlines (aircraft leases, airport gates, pilot salaries, largely fixed regardless of how full the planes are), heavy manufacturing (factories, expensive machinery) — are considered financially RISKIER than industries with low fixed costs and mostly variable costs, like a consulting or staffing firm (where the majority of costs are salaries that can, in principle, be scaled up or down with headcount and billable engagements) — but that same high fixed-cost structure also means airlines and manufacturers can see EBITDA margins expand dramatically during a growth phase, since each additional unit of revenue contributes almost pure profit once the fixed costs are already covered ("operating leverage kicking in," in common industry language).',
          core: 'Fixed costs stay constant regardless of volume; variable costs scale with volume. HIGH operating leverage (a large share of fixed costs) amplifies operating income swings in BOTH directions relative to revenue — magnifying gains during growth (EBITDA margin expansion, since fixed costs do not grow) but also magnifying losses during a downturn (fixed costs still must be paid even as revenue shrinks). This is why high-fixed-cost businesses are both riskier and more profitable in expansion than low-fixed-cost businesses.',
          when: 'Any question about how a change in revenue translates into a DIFFERENT-sized change in profit, or about why certain industries are considered structurally riskier (or more profitable in growth) than others.',
          formulas: ['Degree of operating leverage (DOL) ≈ % change in operating income / % change in revenue.', 'Contribution margin = Revenue − Variable Costs (the amount left over to cover fixed costs and then generate profit).', 'Operating income = Contribution margin − Fixed Costs, meaning fixed costs are a constant SUBTRACTION regardless of volume, while contribution margin scales directly with volume.'],
          intuition: 'Think of fixed costs as a hurdle that must be cleared before ANY profit begins to show — once that hurdle (covering the fixed costs) is cleared, additional revenue drops through to profit almost entirely unimpeded (since variable costs are the only remaining drag), which is exactly why growth feels so powerfully profitable for a high-fixed-cost business, and exactly why a revenue shortfall feels so painfully costly for the same business — the hurdle does not get any lower just because revenue fell short of clearing it.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'An airline has $800m of fixed costs (aircraft leases, ground infrastructure, base crew salaries) and variable costs of $40 per passenger. It currently flies 20 million passengers at an average fare of $120, for revenue of $2,400m. Variable costs = 20m×$40 = $800m. Operating income = 2,400 − 800 (variable) − 800 (fixed) = $800m.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'The same airline sees passenger volume rise 10% to 22 million, at the same $120 fare and $40 variable cost per passenger (fixed costs unchanged at $800m — the same planes and infrastructure serve more passengers). New revenue = 22m×$120 = $2,640m (a 10% increase). New variable costs = 22m×$40 = $880m. New operating income = 2,640 − 880 − 800 = $960m — UP from $800m, a 20% increase in operating income from only a 10% increase in revenue. This 2x amplification (DOL ≈ 20%/10% = 2.0) is operating leverage at work: the $800m of fixed costs did not grow at all, so nearly all of the extra revenue (net of the modest variable cost) dropped straight through to profit.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Now suppose instead that passenger volume FALLS 10% to 18 million, with fares and variable costs unchanged. New revenue = 18m×$120 = $2,160m (a 10% decrease). New variable costs = 18m×$40 = $720m. New operating income = 2,160 − 720 − 800 = $640m — DOWN from $800m, a 20% DECREASE in operating income from only a 10% decrease in revenue — the exact same 2x amplification factor, now working painfully in the opposite direction. This symmetry is the essential lesson: the same operating leverage that doubled the percentage GAIN in the growth scenario also doubles the percentage LOSS in the downturn scenario, which is precisely why high-fixed-cost businesses are considered structurally riskier, not just structurally more profitable.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Assuming high operating leverage is purely a positive attribute ("more profitable") without recognising it amplifies losses just as powerfully as it amplifies gains — a high-fixed-cost business is a genuinely higher-risk business, not simply a higher-reward one, and the SAME multiplier applies whether revenue moves up or down.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'A "fixed cost" is one that:', options: ['Scales up and down directly with production volume', 'Stays roughly the same regardless of production or sales volume', 'Only exists for manufacturing companies', 'Is always larger than variable costs'], a: 1, why: 'Fixed costs (like rent or base salaries) are owed regardless of how much is actually produced or sold.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'A company with HIGH operating leverage will see operating income:', options: ['Move by roughly the same percentage as revenue, always', 'Move by a LARGER percentage than revenue, in whichever direction revenue moves', 'Never change regardless of revenue', 'Only increase, never decrease'], a: 1, why: 'High operating leverage amplifies the percentage swing in operating income relative to revenue, in BOTH directions.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Fixed costs are $500m. Variable cost is $30/unit. At 10m units sold and a $80 price, what is operating income?', options: ['$300m', '$800m', '$200m', '$500m'], a: 0, why: 'Revenue = 10m×$80=$800m. Variable costs = 10m×$30=$300m. Operating income = 800−300−500 = $300m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Using the same company, if unit volume rises 20% to 12m units (same price and variable cost per unit, fixed costs unchanged), what is the new operating income?', options: ['$360m', '$420m', '$300m', '$500m'], a: 1, why: 'Revenue=12m×$80=$960m. Variable=12m×$30=$360m. Operating income=960−360−500=$420m — a jump from $300m to $420m, a 40% increase from only a 20% revenue increase.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why are airlines and heavy manufacturers generally considered financially RISKIER than a consulting firm with mostly variable (salary-based, scalable) costs?', options: ['Airlines and manufacturers always have lower revenue', 'Their high fixed-cost structure means a revenue decline translates into a much LARGER percentage decline in operating income, since fixed costs must still be paid in full', 'Consulting firms have no costs at all', 'There is no real difference in risk between these business models'], a: 1, why: 'High fixed costs amplify the pain of a revenue shortfall, since those costs do not shrink along with the lower revenue.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why does a high-fixed-cost business often see EBITDA MARGIN expand as it grows revenue, even with no change in pricing or variable unit costs?', options: ['EBITDA margin cannot expand from revenue growth alone', 'Because fixed costs do not grow with volume, so each additional dollar of revenue (net of variable costs) drops through to profit at a higher rate than the business\'s existing average margin, pulling the overall margin upward', 'Because variable costs always fall as volume rises', 'Because taxes decrease as revenue increases'], a: 1, why: 'With fixed costs held constant, incremental revenue contributes disproportionately to profit, mechanically expanding the overall margin as volume grows.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-H =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-H', topic: 'DCF Valuation', title: 'Investment decision criteria: IRR, and choosing between WACC and APV', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibh1', name: 'IRR: the discount rate that makes a project break even, and why it can mislead',
          primer: 'Before IRR can make any sense, "NPV" (Net Present Value) needs to be defined precisely first: NPV is the sum of ALL of a project\'s cash flows, each individually discounted back to today\'s value using a chosen discount rate, and then netted against the initial investment — in short, NPV answers "after accounting for the time value of money, does this project create MORE value than it costs, at this specific discount rate?" A positive NPV means the project is expected to generate more value than the discount rate demands as compensation for the risk and the passage of time; a negative NPV means it destroys value relative to that same hurdle. "IRR" (Internal Rate of Return) is then defined as the SPECIFIC discount rate at which a project\'s NPV comes out to EXACTLY zero — in other words, IRR is the break-even discount rate: the annual rate of return a project would need to be discounted at for its costs and benefits to exactly offset. This gives IRR an intuitive, practical decision rule: compare the project\'s IRR to the company\'s actual cost of capital (its WACC, or more informally its "hurdle rate") — if IRR EXCEEDS the hurdle rate, the project is expected to earn MORE than what it costs the company to raise the capital for it (equivalent to a positive NPV at that hurdle rate), so it should generally be pursued; if IRR is BELOW the hurdle rate, the project destroys value and should generally be rejected. IRR, however, has a genuine mathematical weakness worth understanding precisely: if a project\'s cash flows change SIGN more than once over its life (for example: an initial cash OUTFLOW, followed by cash INFLOWS, followed by another large cash OUTFLOW later — perhaps a major decommissioning or clean-up cost) the underlying equation that defines IRR can have MULTIPLE valid solutions (multiple different discount rates that each independently make NPV exactly zero), making "the" IRR ambiguous or even meaningless as a single decision number. In genuinely ambiguous cases like this, or whenever comparing mutually exclusive projects of meaningfully DIFFERENT scale (where a smaller project might show a flashier percentage IRR while a larger project creates more total dollar value), NPV is the more reliable, unambiguous decision criterion, since it directly measures dollar value created rather than a percentage rate that can behave unpredictably. In the specific context of a leveraged buyout, IRR is one of the two central return metrics private equity sponsors track (alongside MOIC, covered in the earlier LBO concepts) — funds typically target IRRs in the region of 20% or higher over a typical multi-year hold, and IRR is particularly useful there because, unlike MOIC, it explicitly accounts for HOW LONG the capital was tied up, letting a fund fairly compare a 2.5× return achieved in 3 years against a 2.5× return achieved in 7 years (the former is a dramatically better IRR, despite the identical MOIC).',
          core: 'NPV = sum of all discounted cash flows minus the initial investment, at a chosen discount rate; positive NPV means value is created. IRR is the specific discount rate that makes NPV exactly zero — the project\'s break-even rate of return. Decision rule: IRR > hurdle rate (WACC) → invest; IRR < hurdle rate → reject. IRR can be misleading (multiple valid IRRs) when cash flows change sign more than once, and can mislead when comparing differently-scaled projects — NPV is the more reliable criterion in those cases.',
          when: 'Any question asking whether a project or investment should be pursued based on comparing a rate of return to a hurdle rate, or any question about the specific weaknesses of IRR as a decision tool.',
          formulas: ['NPV = Σ [CFₜ / (1+r)ᵗ] − Initial Investment, summed over each period t, at discount rate r.', 'IRR is defined as the specific value of r that makes NPV = 0 for a given set of cash flows.', 'Decision rule: IRR > WACC (or hurdle rate) ⇒ invest (equivalent to NPV > 0 at that hurdle rate); IRR < WACC ⇒ reject.', 'For a single lump-sum investment held n years with no interim cash flows: IRR = (ending value / beginning value)^(1/n) − 1 (identical to the MOIC-to-IRR conversion used in LBO analysis).'],
          intuition: 'IRR answers "what rate of return am I effectively earning on this specific investment?" — and comparing that earned rate against the rate you actually have to PAY for capital (the hurdle rate) tells you directly whether the investment is a net win or a net loss, in percentage terms, even before computing a single dollar figure.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A project requires a $100 initial investment and returns $115 after exactly one year, with no other cash flows. What discount rate makes NPV=0? NPV = −100 + 115/(1+r) = 0 → 115/(1+r) = 100 → (1+r) = 1.15 → r = 15%. The IRR is 15%. If the company\'s hurdle rate (WACC) is 10%, this project should be pursued, since 15% > 10%.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A project has IRR of 8%. The company\'s WACC is 12%. Should the project be pursued? No — the project\'s expected rate of return (8%) is LOWER than what it costs the company to raise capital (12%), meaning the project is expected to destroy value relative to its cost of capital, even if it looks "profitable" in a simple, undiscounted sense (total cash in might still exceed total cash out).' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A project has cash flows of −$100 (today), +$300 (year 1), and −$200 (year 2) — notice the sign changes TWICE (negative, then positive, then negative again), which can be a signature of multiple valid IRRs. Solving for r such that NPV=0 in this case can genuinely produce more than one mathematically valid answer (for instance, this specific cash flow pattern has two IRRs, at r=0% and r=100%), making "the IRR" ambiguous as a single decision number — a project manager relying on IRR alone here could easily be misled depending on which root their software happens to report. The safer approach: compute NPV directly at the company\'s actual hurdle rate and use its sign (positive or negative) as the decision criterion instead, sidestepping the multiple-IRR ambiguity entirely.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Treating IRR as always reliable, especially for projects with unconventional (multiple sign-change) cash flow patterns, where more than one mathematically valid IRR can exist — and comparing the raw IRR PERCENTAGES of two mutually exclusive projects of very different SCALE without also checking their NPV in dollar terms, since a smaller project can show a flashier IRR while creating meaningfully less total value than a larger one.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'IRR is defined as:', options: ['The company\'s cost of capital', 'The specific discount rate that makes a project\'s NPV exactly equal to zero', 'The average of all a project\'s cash flows', 'The project\'s total profit, undiscounted'], a: 1, why: 'IRR is precisely the break-even discount rate for the project\'s cash flows.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'A project has IRR of 14%, and the company\'s hurdle rate (WACC) is 9%. What is the standard decision?', options: ['Reject the project, since 14% seems too high to be realistic', 'Pursue the project, since its IRR exceeds the hurdle rate, implying it creates value above its cost of capital', 'It is impossible to decide without more information', 'Always reject if IRR exceeds 10% for any reason'], a: 1, why: 'IRR > hurdle rate is the standard, direct signal that a project is expected to create value.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A project requires $200 today and returns $230 in exactly one year, with no other cash flows. What is its IRR?', options: ['10%', '15%', '30%', '23%'], a: 1, why: '−200 + 230/(1+r) = 0 → 1+r = 1.15 → r = 15%.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'What is the essential definitional difference between NPV and IRR?', options: ['They are exactly the same concept with different names', 'NPV is a dollar amount of value created at a CHOSEN discount rate; IRR is the specific discount rate that would make that dollar value exactly zero', 'IRR is always more accurate than NPV', 'NPV can only be computed for projects with a single cash flow'], a: 1, why: 'NPV outputs a dollar figure given a chosen rate; IRR instead solves for the rate that zeroes out that dollar figure.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A project\'s cash flows change sign more than once over its life (e.g. outflow, then inflow, then a large outflow again). What problem can this create for IRR specifically?', options: ['No problem at all — IRR always works perfectly regardless of cash flow pattern', 'The IRR equation can have MULTIPLE mathematically valid solutions, making a single "the IRR" figure ambiguous or misleading', 'NPV becomes impossible to compute in this case', 'The project automatically becomes unprofitable'], a: 1, why: 'Multiple sign changes can produce multiple valid roots to the IRR equation, undermining IRR as a single, reliable decision number in that specific case.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why is IRR, alongside MOIC, one of the two central metrics PE sponsors track for LBO returns, rather than relying on MOIC alone?', options: ['IRR and MOIC always give identical rankings, so tracking both is redundant', 'IRR explicitly accounts for HOW LONG capital was tied up, letting a fund fairly compare returns achieved over different hold periods — unlike MOIC, which ignores time entirely', 'MOIC is illegal to use in private equity', 'IRR ignores the size of the initial investment entirely'], a: 1, why: 'A given MOIC achieved faster represents a much better IRR — since IRR is time-sensitive and MOIC is not, tracking both gives a fuller picture of return quality.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibh2', name: 'APV versus WACC: two genuinely different ways to value a company with debt',
          primer: 'The WACC-based DCF approach, covered earlier in this track, values a company by discounting its projected free cash flows at a single BLENDED discount rate that already mixes in the cost of both equity and debt, weighted by the company\'s capital structure — this single-rate approach implicitly assumes the company\'s capital structure (its proportion of debt versus equity) stays roughly CONSTANT throughout the projection period, since WACC is a snapshot calculation based on today\'s (or a targeted long-run) mix of debt and equity. "APV" (Adjusted Present Value) takes a fundamentally different, two-step approach for situations where that "stable capital structure" assumption breaks down. Step one: value the company\'s operations as if it had NO debt at all (a hypothetical "unlevered," all-equity version of the business), discounting its free cash flows at the UNLEVERED cost of capital — this produces the "unlevered value," capturing pure operating value with no financing effects mixed in. Step two: separately calculate the PRESENT VALUE of the "interest tax shield" — the extra cash benefit the company gets EACH YEAR because interest payments on its actual debt are tax-deductible (interest expense × tax rate, the same mechanic as the depreciation tax shield, applied to interest instead) — and discount that separate stream of tax-shield benefits back to today (often at the cost of debt, since the tax shield\'s risk is closely tied to the debt payments generating it). APV = unlevered value + PV of the interest tax shield: two separate, explicitly computed pieces, added together. Why does this separation matter? In a leveraged buyout, the amount of debt outstanding — and therefore the SIZE of the interest tax shield — changes dramatically and PREDICTABLY every year, as the company aggressively pays down debt from free cash flow (the "deleveraging" driver of LBO returns covered earlier). Recomputing a brand-new blended WACC every single year to reflect this constantly shifting structure would be impractical and circular; APV sidesteps the problem by valuing the tax shield as its own explicit stream, cleanly reflecting a shrinking debt balance year by year. This is why a standard IB DCF for an established public company — whose capital structure is reasonably stable — typically uses WACC (simpler, industry-standard), while a PE analyst modeling an LBO — where leverage is deliberately, predictably reduced over the hold — often prefers APV, since it isolates the debt-paydown/tax-shield effect as its own distinct, transparent value driver.',
          core: 'WACC-based DCF blends the cost of equity and debt into ONE discount rate, implicitly assuming a roughly stable capital structure. APV instead separates value into two explicit pieces: (1) the unlevered value of the business (discounted at the unlevered cost of capital, ignoring debt entirely) plus (2) the present value of the interest tax shield (computed and discounted separately). APV is preferred when capital structure changes significantly and predictably over time (like an LBO with aggressive, scheduled debt paydown), since it avoids having to recompute WACC every single year.',
          when: 'Any question asking when to use APV instead of WACC, or asking you to explain the two-step mechanics of an APV valuation.',
          formulas: ['APV = Unlevered value of the business + PV of the interest tax shield.', 'Unlevered value = PV of free cash flows, discounted at the UNLEVERED cost of capital (the required return on an all-equity version of the business).', 'Interest tax shield in a given year = interest expense × tax rate (identical mechanic to the depreciation tax shield, applied to interest instead).', 'PV of the tax shield = sum of each year\'s tax shield, individually discounted back to today (commonly at the cost of debt, given the tax shield\'s risk is closely linked to the debt payments generating it).'],
          intuition: 'WACC bakes the financing benefit of debt directly INTO a single discount rate and applies it uniformly to every cash flow; APV instead keeps the "pure business" value and the "financing benefit of debt" as two separate, individually visible line items added together — which is far more useful when the amount of debt (and therefore the size of that financing benefit) is scheduled to change substantially over time, as in an LBO.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company\'s unlevered free cash flows, discounted at its 10% unlevered cost of capital, produce an unlevered value of $800m. Separately, the present value of its interest tax shields over the same period is calculated at $60m. APV = 800 + 60 = $860m.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'An LBO target has debt of $400m in year 1, paid down to $250m by year 3 as the company generates free cash flow. The interest expense — and therefore the interest tax shield — SHRINKS every single year as the debt balance falls. A WACC-based approach would need an entirely new blended WACC calculated for EACH year to reflect the shifting, ever-smaller proportion of debt in the capital structure — a genuinely impractical, circular exercise (WACC for year 2 partly depends on the results of year 1\'s model). APV sidesteps this: it values the business unlevered ONCE (using a single, stable unlevered cost of capital that has nothing to do with the debt schedule), then separately, explicitly models each year\'s shrinking tax shield as its own line item and discounts it back — cleanly handling the year-by-year change in debt without ever needing to recompute a blended discount rate.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Why might a bank\'s equity research team, valuing a stable, mature public company with a long-run target capital structure the company actively manages toward, reasonably prefer WACC over APV? Because that company\'s capital structure genuinely IS expected to stay roughly stable (management targets a consistent debt-to-equity mix over time, unlike an LBO\'s scheduled aggressive deleveraging), so WACC\'s implicit "stable capital structure" assumption is a reasonable approximation of reality here, not a meaningful source of error — and WACC has the added practical benefit of being the industry-standard approach, making the resulting valuation directly comparable to how the company\'s peers are typically valued by other analysts using the same convention.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Using a single, static WACC to value a company whose capital structure is scheduled to change dramatically and predictably (like an LBO with aggressive debt paydown) — this bakes in a snapshot assumption that becomes progressively less accurate every year as leverage actually falls, systematically distorting the valuation; APV is specifically designed to avoid this by separating operating value from the (correctly time-varying) tax shield.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'What does APV stand for, and what are its two main components?', options: ['Average Present Value; total assets plus total liabilities', 'Adjusted Present Value; unlevered business value plus the present value of the interest tax shield', 'Annual Percentage Value; revenue plus costs', 'Adjusted Portfolio Value; equity value plus debt value'], a: 1, why: 'APV explicitly separates a company\'s value into its pure operating (unlevered) value and the separately-valued benefit of its debt-driven tax shield.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'The WACC-based DCF approach implicitly assumes:', options: ['The company will never generate any free cash flow', 'The company\'s capital structure (debt versus equity mix) stays roughly stable over the projection period', 'The company has no debt at all', 'Interest expense is never tax-deductible'], a: 1, why: 'WACC is a single blended rate based on a given capital structure; using it consistently across a multi-year forecast implicitly assumes that mix does not change dramatically.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A company\'s unlevered value is $500m, and the PV of its interest tax shield is $35m. What is its APV?', options: ['$465m', '$535m', '$500m', '$35m'], a: 1, why: 'APV = unlevered value + PV of tax shield = 500 + 35 = $535m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'The interest tax shield in a given year is calculated as:', options: ['Interest expense divided by the tax rate', 'Interest expense multiplied by the tax rate', 'Interest expense multiplied by (1 − tax rate)', 'Revenue multiplied by the tax rate'], a: 1, why: 'This mirrors the depreciation tax shield mechanic: the tax-deductible expense (here, interest) multiplied directly by the tax rate gives the cash tax saving.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why would a WACC-based DCF be especially impractical for valuing an LBO with a debt schedule that pays down aggressively over the hold period?', options: ['WACC-based DCF is never impractical for any situation', 'The capital structure — and therefore the correct WACC — would need to be recomputed for every single year as debt shrinks, an impractical and circular exercise', 'LBOs never generate any free cash flow', 'Interest is not tax-deductible in an LBO'], a: 1, why: 'A rapidly and predictably changing capital structure undermines the "roughly stable mix" assumption a single WACC relies on, making year-by-year WACC recalculation impractical.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why does a PE analyst modeling an LBO often prefer APV specifically, beyond just avoiding the WACC-recalculation problem?', options: ['APV is required by law for all LBO models', 'APV explicitly isolates the tax-shield/deleveraging benefit as its own visible line item, consistent with treating debt paydown as one of the distinct, separately-trackable drivers of LBO equity returns', 'APV always produces a higher valuation than WACC', 'APV ignores free cash flow entirely'], a: 1, why: 'Separating the unlevered business value from the tax-shield benefit directly mirrors how LBO returns are already decomposed into distinct drivers (EBITDA growth, multiple change, deleveraging), making APV a naturally consistent framework for this specific context.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== IB-I =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'IB-I', topic: 'Equity & Capital Markets', title: 'Beta, risk, short selling, liquidity and buybacks: how the market prices and moves equity', track: 'ib', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ibi1', name: 'Levered and unlevered beta: stripping out capital structure to compare companies fairly',
          primer: '"Beta" measures how much a stock\'s returns move relative to the overall market\'s returns — a beta of 1.0 means the stock tends to move in lockstep with the market; a beta of 1.5 means it tends to swing about 50% MORE than the market (up or down); a beta of 0.6 means it swings about 40% LESS. Beta is normally estimated by looking at a company\'s actual historical stock returns, which reflects that specific company\'s ACTUAL capital structure — how much debt it carries. This creates a subtle problem when you want to use a set of comparable companies to estimate the cost of equity for a target company: debt itself makes a stock riskier (fixed interest payments must be paid regardless of how the business is doing, so equity holders bear amplified swings), so a peer with lots of debt will show a HIGHER beta than an otherwise-identical peer with little debt, purely because of financing choices, not because its underlying business is riskier. To fix this, analysts "unlever" each peer\'s observed (levered) beta, stripping OUT the effect of that peer\'s own specific debt load, to isolate a pure measure of the peer\'s BUSINESS risk alone — called the "unlevered beta" or "asset beta." The standard tool for this is the Hamada equation: unlevered beta = levered beta ÷ [1 + (1−tax rate)×(Debt/Equity)]. Once every peer\'s beta has been unlevered this way, they become directly comparable (pure business risk, no financing noise), so an analyst can average or take the median of the peer unlevered betas. The final step reverses the process for the TARGET company specifically: "relever" that peer-average unlevered beta using the TARGET\'s OWN debt-to-equity ratio (not any peer\'s), via relevered beta = unlevered beta × [1 + (1−tax rate)×(target\'s Debt/Equity)] — producing a beta that reflects the peer group\'s business risk, but the target\'s OWN specific financing structure, which is exactly the beta that belongs in the target\'s own CAPM cost of equity calculation.',
          core: 'Unlever each peer\'s beta to strip out its own debt load (pure business risk): βu = βl / [1+(1−t)(D/E)]. Average the unlevered peer betas, then relever using the TARGET\'s own D/E: βl(target) = βu × [1+(1−t)(D/E)target]. This produces a beta with peer business risk but the target\'s own financing structure — the correct input for the target\'s cost of equity.',
          when: 'Any question about comparing beta across companies with different leverage, or building a target company\'s cost of equity from a peer set using the "comps beta" approach.',
          formulas: ['Unlever: βu = βl / [1 + (1−t) × (D/E)], where t = tax rate, D/E = that company\'s own debt-to-equity ratio.', 'Relever: βl = βu × [1 + (1−t) × (D/E)], applying the TARGET company\'s own D/E to a peer-derived unlevered beta.', 'Workflow: unlever each peer → average/median the unlevered betas → relever once, at the target\'s own D/E.'],
          intuition: 'Unlevering beta is like removing a company\'s "financing costume" to see its bare business risk underneath; relevering is putting the TARGET\'s own costume back on that bare risk measure — you borrow the business-risk insight from peers, but dress it in the target\'s own capital structure.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A peer has a levered beta of 1.20, a debt-to-equity ratio of 0.50, and a 25% tax rate. Unlevered beta = 1.20 / [1 + (1−0.25)×0.50] = 1.20 / [1 + 0.375] = 1.20/1.375 ≈ 0.873.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Three peers have levered betas of 1.10, 1.30, and 1.00, with D/E ratios of 0.30, 0.80, and 0.10 respectively, all at a 25% tax rate. Peer 1: 1.10/[1+0.75×0.30] = 1.10/1.225 ≈ 0.898. Peer 2: 1.30/[1+0.75×0.80] = 1.30/1.60 = 0.8125. Peer 3: 1.00/[1+0.75×0.10] = 1.00/1.075 ≈ 0.930. Average unlevered beta = (0.898+0.8125+0.930)/3 ≈ 0.880.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Using that peer-average unlevered beta of 0.880, the target company itself has a D/E ratio of 0.60 and faces a 25% tax rate. Relevered beta = 0.880 × [1 + 0.75×0.60] = 0.880 × 1.45 ≈ 1.276. If the risk-free rate is 4% and the equity risk premium is 5.5%, the target\'s cost of equity = 4% + 1.276×5.5% ≈ 11.02%. Notice the target ends up with a HIGHER beta (1.276) than the peer average (0.880) purely because the target itself carries more relative leverage (D/E of 0.60) than the unlevered baseline — this is exactly the leverage-driven risk amplification the Hamada equation is designed to capture correctly.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Averaging peers\' raw LEVERED betas directly, without unlevering first — this bakes each peer\'s own arbitrary debt load into the average, contaminating the comparison with financing noise rather than pure business risk. A second common error: relevering using a PEER\'s D/E instead of the TARGET\'s own D/E — the whole point of the final step is to reflect the target\'s own capital structure, not any single peer\'s.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Why can you not simply average peers\' raw (levered) betas when building a target\'s cost of equity?', options: ['Averaging is mathematically impossible with more than 2 peers', 'Each peer\'s levered beta reflects its own debt load, which contaminates the comparison with financing differences rather than pure business risk', 'Beta is never comparable across companies for any reason', 'Levered beta is always lower than unlevered beta'], a: 1, why: 'Debt amplifies equity risk, so peers with different leverage show different betas for reasons unrelated to their underlying business risk — unlevering removes this financing noise first.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'The Hamada equation to UNLEVER a beta is:', options: ['βu = βl × [1+(1−t)(D/E)]', 'βu = βl / [1+(1−t)(D/E)]', 'βu = βl + (1−t)(D/E)', 'βu = βl × t × (D/E)'], a: 1, why: 'Unlevering divides the levered beta by [1+(1−t)(D/E)], removing the effect of that company\'s own leverage.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A peer has a levered beta of 1.50, D/E of 1.0, tax rate 20%. What is its unlevered beta?', options: ['1.50', '0.833', '1.80', '0.667'], a: 1, why: 'βu = 1.50 / [1+(1−0.20)×1.0] = 1.50/1.80 ≈ 0.833.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A target has an unlevered (peer-average) beta of 0.90, a D/E of 0.40, and a 25% tax rate. What is its relevered beta?', options: ['0.90', '1.17', '0.63', '1.35'], a: 1, why: 'βl = 0.90 × [1+(1−0.25)×0.40] = 0.90×1.30 = 1.17.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Two companies have IDENTICAL underlying businesses (same unlevered beta), but Company A carries much more debt than Company B. Which has the higher levered beta, and why?', options: ['Company B, because less debt always means more risk', 'Company A, because more debt amplifies equity risk for a given amount of business risk', 'They are always equal regardless of leverage', 'Levered beta has nothing to do with debt levels'], a: 1, why: 'The relevering formula shows beta rising as D/E rises (holding unlevered beta fixed) — more leverage means equity holders absorb more amplified swings for the same underlying business risk.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why must the FINAL relevering step use the TARGET company\'s own D/E, not a peer\'s?', options: ['It genuinely does not matter which D/E is used', 'The goal is a beta reflecting peer-derived BUSINESS risk combined with the TARGET\'s own actual financing structure, since that is the company actually being valued', 'Peers never have a D/E ratio', 'The target\'s D/E is always identical to the peer average by definition'], a: 1, why: 'The whole exercise unlevers to get pure business risk from peers, then relevers specifically at the target\'s own leverage, since that is the capital structure the target\'s actual cost of equity must reflect.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibi2', name: 'Systematic versus unsystematic risk: why only market-wide risk earns extra return',
          primer: 'Every stock\'s total risk (the variability of its returns) can be split conceptually into two very different kinds. "Systematic risk" (also called market risk) comes from forces that affect essentially ALL companies simultaneously to some degree — a recession, a broad shift in interest rates, a war, a pandemic — and because it hits the whole market at once, it cannot be escaped by simply owning many different stocks. "Unsystematic risk" (also called idiosyncratic, firm-specific, or diversifiable risk) comes from events specific to one company or narrow industry — a factory fire, a product recall, a lawsuit, a single competitor stealing market share — and because these events are largely UNCORRELATED across different companies, an investor holding a broad, diversified portfolio of many stocks finds that one company\'s bad luck tends to be offset by another company\'s good luck, so the idiosyncratic swings roughly cancel out at the portfolio level. This is the mathematical foundation of diversification: as you add more uncorrelated holdings to a portfolio, unsystematic risk shrinks toward zero, but systematic risk remains no matter how many stocks you hold, since it affects them all together. This distinction directly explains WHY the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) only compensates investors for systematic risk: since unsystematic risk can be eliminated for free simply by diversifying, the market does not need to pay investors extra return for bearing a risk they could have avoided at no cost — only the risk that CANNOT be diversified away deserves a return premium. CAPM captures exactly this systematic-risk exposure with beta, giving the full formula: cost of equity = risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium, where the risk-free rate is the return on an essentially default-free asset (like a government bond), the equity risk premium is the extra return investors demand for holding the market\'s average level of systematic risk over that risk-free rate, and beta scales that premium up or down based on how much MORE or LESS systematic risk this specific stock carries relative to the market average (beta of exactly 1.0).',
          core: 'Systematic risk (market-wide, e.g. recessions, rate changes) cannot be diversified away; unsystematic risk (firm-specific, e.g. a lawsuit) can be, since it is uncorrelated across companies and roughly cancels out in a diversified portfolio. CAPM only compensates investors for systematic risk (via beta), since unsystematic risk is avoidable for free through diversification. CAPM: cost of equity = risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium.',
          when: 'Any question about why diversification reduces risk, why CAPM only prices beta/systematic risk, or asking you to classify a specific risk as systematic or unsystematic.',
          formulas: ['CAPM: Cost of equity = Rf + β × (Rm − Rf), where Rf = risk-free rate, Rm = expected market return, (Rm−Rf) = equity risk premium (ERP).', 'β = 1.0 means the same systematic risk as the market; β > 1.0 amplifies market moves; β < 1.0 dampens them.', 'Total risk = systematic risk + unsystematic risk; diversification reduces only the unsystematic component toward zero as portfolio breadth increases.'],
          intuition: 'Imagine flipping many independent coins versus watching one shared weather forecast: each individual coin flip (unsystematic risk) is unpredictable on its own, but averaged across thousands of flips the noise washes out; the weather forecast (systematic risk) applies to every coin-flipper standing outside at once, and no amount of averaging across people removes it — CAPM only pays you for standing out in that shared weather, not for any single coin flip.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'An investor holds a single stock in a pharmaceutical company. A clinical trial for the company\'s lead drug unexpectedly fails, and the stock drops 40%. This is a clear example of unsystematic (firm-specific) risk — it has essentially nothing to do with the broader market, and an investor who instead held 50 different pharma and non-pharma stocks would barely notice the effect on their overall portfolio.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A stock has a beta of 1.4. The risk-free rate is 3.5%, and the equity risk premium is 5%. Cost of equity = 3.5% + 1.4×5% = 3.5% + 7% = 10.5%. This stock is expected to (and required to, per CAPM) deliver a higher return than the market average specifically because it carries 40% MORE systematic risk than the market, not because of any firm-specific factor.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Two companies have identical unsystematic risk profiles (each has one large, idiosyncratic lawsuit risk of similar size) but very different betas: Company X has beta 0.7, Company Y has beta 1.6. Under CAPM, which one requires a higher expected return, and why does the lawsuit risk itself not directly affect that answer? Company Y requires the higher return (its cost of equity is driven up purely by its higher beta), while the lawsuit risk — being unsystematic — does not enter the CAPM formula for either company at all, since a diversified investor could largely diversify it away. This illustrates the sometimes counterintuitive real-world implication: a company can face a genuinely large, scary company-specific risk yet not have that risk directly priced into its CAPM-implied cost of equity, precisely because CAPM assumes the marginal investor is well-diversified.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Assuming a large, scary firm-specific risk (a lawsuit, a product failure) must automatically raise a stock\'s CAPM-implied cost of equity — under CAPM\'s core assumption of a diversified investor, only SYSTEMATIC risk (captured by beta) is priced; unsystematic risk, however large in isolation, is assumed to be diversifiable away and is not directly compensated.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'Which of these is an example of SYSTEMATIC risk?', options: ['A single company\'s factory catching fire', 'A broad, economy-wide recession affecting nearly all companies', 'One company\'s CEO resigning unexpectedly', 'A single product recall at one company'], a: 1, why: 'A recession affects essentially the whole market simultaneously, making it undiversifiable — the defining feature of systematic risk.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why can unsystematic risk be reduced through diversification, while systematic risk cannot?', options: ['Unsystematic risk is always smaller in size', 'Unsystematic risk is firm-specific and largely uncorrelated across companies, so it roughly cancels out across a broad portfolio; systematic risk affects all companies together and does not cancel out', 'Systematic risk does not actually exist', 'Diversification eliminates all risk of every kind'], a: 1, why: 'Uncorrelated firm-specific shocks average out across many holdings; market-wide shocks hit every holding at once and cannot be averaged away.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A stock has beta 0.8. Risk-free rate is 4%, equity risk premium is 6%. What is its CAPM cost of equity?', options: ['10%', '8.8%', '4.8%', '6%'], a: 1, why: 'Cost of equity = 4% + 0.8×6% = 4%+4.8% = 8.8%.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Why does CAPM compensate investors for systematic risk but not unsystematic risk?', options: ['Unsystematic risk is not real risk', 'Because unsystematic risk can be eliminated for free through diversification, so the market does not need to pay a premium for bearing a risk an investor could have avoided at no cost', 'CAPM actually compensates for both equally', 'Systematic risk is always smaller than unsystematic risk'], a: 1, why: 'A risk that can be diversified away for free is not one the market needs to reward — CAPM assumes investors hold diversified portfolios, so only the undiversifiable (systematic) portion earns a return premium.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A company faces a large, company-specific lawsuit risk but has a low beta of 0.6. What does CAPM imply about its required return relative to a company with beta 1.3 and no notable lawsuit risk?', options: ['The company with the lawsuit requires a higher return, because of the lawsuit', 'The company with beta 1.3 requires the higher CAPM return, since the lawsuit risk (unsystematic) is not directly priced, while the higher beta (systematic risk) is', 'Both require exactly the same return', 'CAPM cannot be applied when a lawsuit is present'], a: 1, why: 'CAPM prices only beta/systematic risk; the higher-beta company requires more expected return under CAPM regardless of the lower-beta company\'s firm-specific lawsuit exposure.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'An investor holds only 2 stocks. Why is their portfolio likely to still show meaningful UNSYSTEMATIC risk, compared to an investor holding 500 stocks?', options: ['Unsystematic risk is identical no matter how many stocks are held', 'With only 2 holdings, firm-specific shocks are far less likely to average out or offset each other than with 500 diverse holdings, so idiosyncratic swings still meaningfully affect the 2-stock portfolio', 'Systematic risk disappears with only 2 stocks', '2 stocks always have zero unsystematic risk by definition'], a: 1, why: 'Diversification benefits scale with the number of sufficiently uncorrelated holdings — a 2-stock portfolio is far too concentrated for firm-specific shocks to reliably cancel out, unlike a much broader 500-stock portfolio.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibi3', name: 'Short selling: profiting when a stock falls, and why the risk is not symmetric',
          primer: 'A normal ("long") stock position profits when the share price RISES: you buy shares, hold them, and later sell at a hopefully higher price. "Short selling" is a way to profit from a share price FALLING instead, and it works through a specific borrowing mechanic. First, the short seller BORROWS shares of a stock (typically arranged through their broker, from another investor who owns the shares and is willing to lend them out for a fee) and immediately SELLS those borrowed shares on the open market at the current price, receiving cash. The short seller now owes the LENDER the shares back, not cash — so at some later point, they must buy an equal number of shares back on the open market (called "covering" the short) and return them to the lender. If the share price has fallen in the meantime, the short seller buys back the shares more cheaply than they sold them for, pocketing the difference as profit; if the price has instead RISEN, they must buy back at a higher price than they sold for, realizing a loss. This is where short selling\'s risk profile becomes fundamentally different from a normal long position: a long position\'s maximum possible loss is capped at 100% of the amount invested (a stock price cannot fall below zero), but a short position\'s maximum possible loss is theoretically UNLIMITED, since a stock\'s price has no upper ceiling — if a heavily shorted stock unexpectedly rallies hard, short sellers rushing to cover (buy back shares to limit their losses) can themselves drive the price even higher, a self-reinforcing spiral called a "short squeeze." Because of this open-ended risk, brokers require short sellers to post "margin" — collateral held against the position, which can trigger a forced margin call (demanding more collateral, or forced covering) if the stock price rises too far. Short selling is used both as an outright bearish bet (believing a stock is overvalued or a company\'s prospects are worsening) and defensively, in "pair trades" or hedges (e.g. going long one company while shorting a close competitor, betting on relative performance rather than the overall market\'s direction).',
          core: 'Short selling: borrow shares, sell them immediately, later buy them back ("cover") and return them to the lender. Profits if the price FALLS (buy back cheaper than sold); loses if the price RISES (buy back more expensively). Unlike a long position (max loss = 100% of investment), a short position has theoretically UNLIMITED maximum loss, since a stock price has no ceiling — this is why margin requirements and margin calls exist, and why a "short squeeze" (rising prices forcing shorts to buy back, pushing prices higher still) is a real risk.',
          when: 'Any question about short-selling mechanics, why short-position risk is asymmetric versus a long position, or short squeezes and margin.',
          formulas: ['Short-sale profit/loss per share = (price sold at) − (price bought back at) [positive if the stock fell, negative if it rose].', 'Long position maximum loss = 100% of amount invested (price floor of zero). Short position maximum loss = theoretically unlimited (no price ceiling).', 'Margin requirement: collateral posted against the short position, which can trigger a margin call as the stock price rises against the position.'],
          intuition: 'A long position is like buying a lottery ticket where the most you can lose is what you paid for it; a short position is like selling insurance against a stock rising — if it rises a LOT, your liability keeps growing with no natural cap, which is exactly why the risk is not a mirror image of going long.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'An investor borrows and sells short 100 shares at $50 each, receiving $5,000. The stock later falls to $35. They buy back 100 shares for $3,500 and return them to the lender. Profit = $5,000 − $3,500 = $1,500 (before any borrow fees).' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Same setup ($50 short-sale price, 100 shares, $5,000 received), but instead the stock RISES to $80. Buying back 100 shares now costs $8,000. Loss = $5,000 − $8,000 = −$3,000, a loss of 60% of the original $5,000 proceeds — and if the stock kept rising further, say to $150, the loss would be $5,000 − $15,000 = −$10,000, already exceeding the original amount involved, illustrating the unlimited-downside property directly (compare: a LONG position of $5,000 could never lose more than the original $5,000, no matter how far the stock fell).' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A stock with heavy short interest (many investors are short) unexpectedly reports strong earnings and jumps 40% in a single day. Many short sellers, facing mounting losses and margin calls, rush to buy back shares to cover their positions and cap further losses — but this wave of buying itself adds MORE demand for the stock, pushing the price up even further, which forces even more shorts to cover, in a reinforcing cycle. Why does this "short squeeze" dynamic specifically require a stock to have been heavily shorted beforehand? Because the squeeze is driven by forced BUYING from short sellers covering their positions — the more shares were sold short (and therefore must eventually be bought back), the larger the pool of forced future buying that a sharp price rise can trigger, which is why short-interest data (how many shares are currently sold short) is closely watched as a squeeze-risk indicator.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Assuming a short position\'s maximum loss is capped at 100%, the same way a long position\'s is — this conflates the two very different risk profiles. A long position\'s loss is bounded because a price cannot fall below zero; a short position\'s loss is UNBOUNDED because a price has no upper limit, and this asymmetry is the single most important fact about short selling.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'The basic mechanic of a short sale is:', options: ['Buy shares first, sell them later', 'Borrow shares, sell them immediately, later buy them back and return them to the lender', 'Buy a put option on the shares', 'Lend your own shares out for a fee'], a: 1, why: 'Short selling begins with borrowing shares (not owning them), selling them immediately, and later repurchasing ("covering") to return to the lender.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'A short position profits when the stock price:', options: ['Rises', 'Falls', 'Stays exactly flat', 'It never profits under any scenario'], a: 1, why: 'The short seller sells first (at the original price) and buys back later — profiting if that later buy-back price is lower.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'An investor shorts 200 shares at $30 each. The stock falls to $22 and they cover. What is the profit (ignoring fees)?', options: ['$1,600', '$6,000', '$4,400', '$1,200'], a: 0, why: 'Proceeds = 200×30 = $6,000. Buyback cost = 200×22 = $4,400. Profit = 6,000−4,400 = $1,600.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Why is a short position\'s maximum possible loss theoretically UNLIMITED, unlike a long position?', options: ['Short sellers are charged unlimited fees', 'A stock price has no upper ceiling, so the cost to buy back shares to cover a short can keep rising indefinitely; a long position\'s loss is capped since price cannot fall below zero', 'This is a myth — both are equally capped at 100%', 'Only short positions are subject to margin calls'], a: 1, why: 'The asymmetry comes directly from the fact that prices are bounded below by zero but have no bound above.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'What specifically triggers a "short squeeze"?', options: ['A stock price falling steadily over many months', 'A sharp price RISE forcing short sellers to buy back shares to limit losses, and that forced buying itself pushes the price up further, forcing even more covering', 'A company issuing new shares', 'Short sellers voluntarily agreeing to buy back shares together'], a: 1, why: 'A short squeeze is a self-reinforcing cycle: rising prices force covering (buying), and that buying adds further upward pressure on price, forcing yet more covering.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why might an investor short one stock while simultaneously going LONG a close competitor, rather than just shorting outright?', options: ['This combination is never used in practice', 'To bet on the RELATIVE performance between the two companies (pair trade), reducing exposure to overall market direction that would affect both stocks similarly', 'Because shorting alone is illegal', 'To guarantee a profit regardless of what happens to either stock'], a: 1, why: 'A long/short pair trade isolates company-specific (relative) views by offsetting much of the shared, market-wide (systematic) risk that would otherwise affect both positions in the same direction.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibi4', name: 'The liquidity premium: why hard-to-sell assets must offer a higher return',
          primer: '"Liquidity" describes how quickly and easily an asset can be converted into cash at close to its true, fair value, without having to accept a steep price discount just to find a buyer fast. Publicly-traded stock is highly liquid: it trades continuously on an exchange, with a visible, up-to-the-second market price, and an investor can typically sell a normal-sized position within seconds at a price very close to that quoted market price. Shares in a PRIVATE company are dramatically less liquid: there is no exchange, no continuously visible price, and finding a willing buyer can take months of searching, negotiation, and expensive legal/banking work — and even then, a buyer of an illiquid stake typically demands a lower price (relative to comparable public-company value) specifically to compensate them for the risk and inconvenience of being stuck holding something they cannot quickly resell if their own circumstances change. This gap — the extra expected return (equivalently, the price discount) that illiquid assets must offer relative to an otherwise-identical liquid asset — is called the "liquidity premium" (or, in private-company valuation specifically, the "discount for lack of marketability," DLOM). It is not a minor technicality: private-company valuations commonly apply illiquidity discounts in a wide range, often cited around 10-30% below what an economically identical public comparable would trade at, precisely to reflect this cost of being locked in. The same logic extends directly into wealth management: illiquid alternative investments (private equity funds, direct real estate, hedge funds with multi-year "lockup" periods that prevent withdrawals) must offer investors a meaningfully higher EXPECTED return than liquid public stocks and bonds to justify tying up capital for years with no ability to exit on demand — if they offered the same expected return as a liquid public stock, no rational investor would accept the illiquidity for free. This is also why liquidity needs are a central part of any client\'s investment suitability assessment: an investor who might need to access their capital on short notice should hold a much smaller allocation to illiquid assets, regardless of how attractive those assets\' expected returns look on paper.',
          core: 'Liquidity = how quickly/easily an asset converts to cash near its fair value. Public equity is highly liquid; private equity stakes and locked-up alternative investments are not. Investors demand a "liquidity premium" (higher expected return / lower price today, commonly a DLOM of roughly 10-30% in private valuations) to compensate for being unable to exit quickly — this applies both to valuing private companies and to why illiquid alternative investments must offer higher expected returns than liquid public markets.',
          when: 'Any question about why private-company valuations trade at a discount to public comparables, why illiquid investments must offer a higher expected return, or client liquidity-needs suitability questions.',
          formulas: ['Illiquidity-adjusted value ≈ comparable public-market value × (1 − DLOM), where DLOM (discount for lack of marketability) is commonly cited in the ~10-30% range.', 'Required return on an illiquid asset ≈ required return on an equivalent liquid asset + liquidity premium (the extra compensation demanded for being unable to exit on demand).'],
          intuition: 'Think of two otherwise-identical apartments, one you could sell tomorrow at a fair market price and one that legally cannot be sold for five years — even if the apartments themselves are physically identical, almost nobody would pay the same price for both, because being LOCKED IN is itself a real cost that has to be compensated somehow, either through a lower purchase price today or a higher expected payoff later.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A public comparable company trades at an equity value of $500m. An economically similar PRIVATE company, otherwise identical in every operating respect, is valued applying a 20% DLOM. Private company value ≈ 500 × (1−0.20) = $400m — purely due to the illiquidity of a private stake, not any difference in the underlying business.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'A liquid public equity index is expected to return 8% per year. An illiquid private equity fund, with a 7-year lockup, is being pitched to a client. If the fund is only expected to return 8.5% per year, is that adequate compensation for the illiquidity? Most institutional investors would say no — an extra 0.5% is a thin liquidity premium for tying up capital for 7 years with no ability to exit, especially when a genuinely comparable premium for multi-year illiquidity is often argued to be several percentage points, not fractions of one.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A wealth manager is assessing two clients with identical net worth and identical target asset allocations on paper. Client A has stable income and no foreseeable large cash needs for 10+ years; Client B may need a large lump sum within 2 years for a business investment. Even with identical target allocations "on paper," why should the wealth manager recommend a meaningfully SMALLER allocation to illiquid alternatives (private equity, locked-up funds) for Client B specifically? Because Client B\'s realistic liquidity NEED is much shorter than the lockup periods those investments impose — if the cash need arrives while the capital is locked up, Client B may be forced into a costly, disadvantageous secondary-market sale (itself typically at another discount) or simply be unable to access the funds at all, a mismatch that the illiquidity premium does not fix, since it compensates for illiquidity in expectation, not for a specific, known, near-term cash need.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Treating illiquid investments as simply "higher expected return, no real downside" without weighing whether the INVESTOR\'s own liquidity needs and time horizon can actually tolerate being locked up — a liquidity premium compensates for the average investor\'s inconvenience over a long horizon, not for an individual client\'s specific, near-term cash need, which a lockup can turn into a genuine problem rather than a mere inconvenience.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'What does "liquidity" describe, in the context of an asset?', options: ['How much the asset is worth in total', 'How quickly and easily the asset can be converted to cash near its fair value', 'How risky the asset\'s cash flows are', 'How much debt the asset\'s owner has'], a: 1, why: 'Liquidity is specifically about the SPEED and EASE of converting to cash without a steep price concession — a separate concept from total value or risk.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why do private-company valuations commonly apply a discount relative to an otherwise-identical public comparable?', options: ['Private companies are always less profitable', 'To compensate for the lack of a ready market — the difficulty and cost of finding a buyer and exiting the investment (the liquidity premium / DLOM)', 'Private companies never pay any taxes', 'This is a legal requirement with no economic basis'], a: 1, why: 'The discount for lack of marketability reflects the genuine economic cost of being unable to sell quickly at a fair price, independent of the underlying business quality.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A public comparable is worth $200m. Applying a 15% DLOM to an economically similar private company, what is its estimated value?', options: ['$230m', '$170m', '$200m', '$185m'], a: 1, why: '200 × (1−0.15) = $170m.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Why must illiquid alternative investments (private equity, locked-up hedge funds) offer a higher EXPECTED return than liquid public markets?', options: ['They are required by regulation to do so', 'If they offered the same expected return as a liquid asset, no rational investor would accept being unable to exit on demand for no extra compensation', 'Illiquid investments are always riskier in every other respect too', 'Expected return has nothing to do with liquidity'], a: 1, why: 'Investors must be compensated specifically for the inconvenience/risk of being locked in — otherwise there is no rational reason to accept illiquidity over an equally-returning liquid alternative.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A client has a genuine, likely need for a large sum of cash within 18 months. Why is a large allocation to a 7-year-lockup private equity fund inappropriate for this client, even if the fund\'s expected return is attractive?', options: ['Private equity funds always perform poorly', 'The lockup could prevent access to capital exactly when the client needs it, and the liquidity premium compensates for illiquidity in general, not for a specific known near-term cash need', 'This client should never invest in anything illiquid under any circumstances', 'The fund\'s expected return is irrelevant to any suitability decision'], a: 1, why: 'A liquidity premium is compensation over a long horizon for the average investor\'s inconvenience — it does not solve a specific, near-term, known liquidity need, which a lockup can turn into a real problem.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Two economically identical businesses, one public and one private, are compared. All else equal, which requires investors to demand a HIGHER expected return, and why?', options: ['The public company, because it is more scrutinized', 'The private company, because its illiquidity (no ready market, harder and slower to exit) demands extra compensation beyond pure business risk', 'Neither — liquidity has no effect on required returns', 'They require identical returns since the underlying businesses are identical'], a: 1, why: 'With identical business risk, the private company\'s illiquidity is the sole remaining differentiator, and it specifically requires a liquidity premium on top of the business-risk-driven return.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ibi5', name: 'Stock buybacks: the mechanics, the EPS effect, and what a buyback signals',
          primer: 'A "stock buyback" (or "share repurchase") is when a company uses its own cash to buy its own shares back from the open market (or sometimes via a formal "tender offer" to shareholders), and those repurchased shares are typically then RETIRED, permanently reducing the total number of shares outstanding. Buybacks are one of two standard ways a company returns capital to shareholders, the other being dividends — but they work very differently: a dividend sends cash directly to every shareholder proportionally; a buyback instead reduces the share count, which mechanically increases each REMAINING shareholder\'s percentage ownership of the company, with no direct cash payment made to them individually. The most heavily tested mechanical consequence of a buyback is its effect on earnings per share (EPS): because EPS = net income ÷ shares outstanding, shrinking the DENOMINATOR (fewer shares) mechanically raises EPS even if net income itself does not grow one bit — this is a critical distinction analysts must always keep in mind: EPS growth driven purely by a shrinking share count is not the same thing as REAL earnings growth from the underlying business improving, and conflating the two can make a stagnating company look like a growing one on a per-share basis. Buybacks also carry a "signaling" dimension: when management chooses to spend the company\'s own cash buying its own stock, it is often interpreted by the market as a signal that management believes the stock is currently UNDERVALUED (since spending cash on an overpriced asset would be a poor use of capital) — this is generally read as a positive signal. But buybacks are not free of criticism: a large buyback program can also signal that management sees no BETTER use for the cash (no attractive new growth investments, acquisitions, or R&D opportunities worth funding instead), which can itself be read as a somewhat negative signal about a company\'s growth prospects; buybacks funded with newly-issued DEBT (rather than existing cash) also increase financial leverage and risk, purely to boost EPS. Buybacks are generally seen as more flexible than dividends (a company can pause a buyback program quietly, whereas cutting a dividend is a much stronger, more visible negative signal to the market) and, in many tax jurisdictions, more tax-efficient for shareholders who do not sell (no cash is received, so no immediate tax event, unlike a dividend which is typically taxed on receipt).',
          core: 'A buyback: company spends cash to repurchase and retire its own shares, shrinking share count. EPS = net income / shares, so a smaller share count mechanically RAISES EPS even with zero real earnings growth — a critical distinction from real business growth. Signaling: buybacks often signal management believes shares are undervalued (positive read) but can also signal a lack of better investment opportunities (negative read); debt-funded buybacks raise leverage/risk purely to lift EPS. Buybacks are more flexible and often more tax-efficient than dividends for non-selling shareholders.',
          when: 'Any question about how a buyback mechanically affects EPS, what a buyback announcement signals about management\'s view, or comparing buybacks to dividends as capital return methods.',
          formulas: ['EPS = Net income / shares outstanding — a buyback lowers the denominator, mechanically raising EPS holding net income constant.', 'New EPS after buyback ≈ Net income / (original shares − shares repurchased).', 'Shares repurchased = dollar amount spent on the buyback / average repurchase price per share.'],
          intuition: 'A buyback is like cutting a pizza into fewer, larger slices without adding any more pizza — every remaining slice-holder gets a bigger piece (higher EPS), but the pizza itself (total earnings) never actually got any bigger; real growth means baking a bigger pizza, not just cutting it differently.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A company has $80m of net income and 40m shares outstanding, EPS = $2.00. It spends $100m buying back shares at $25 each, repurchasing 100/25 = 4m shares, leaving 36m shares. Assuming net income is unchanged, new EPS = 80/36 ≈ $2.22 — EPS rose about 11%, entirely from the shrinking share count, with zero actual earnings growth.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Company management announces a large buyback program, stating publicly that they believe the stock is significantly undervalued relative to the company\'s intrinsic worth. How should the market typically interpret this? As a broadly positive signal — management, who presumably has the best inside view of the business\'s prospects, is voluntarily choosing to spend the company\'s cash on its own stock rather than on any alternative use, which is a costly, credible way of expressing confidence (spending real money "puts skin in the game" behind the claim, unlike a mere public statement that costs nothing to make).' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A mature company with slowing growth borrows $500m of new debt specifically to fund an aggressive buyback program, boosting EPS by 15% with no change to underlying operating performance. An analyst flags two separate concerns: first, this EPS growth is entirely financial engineering — it did not come from the business actually earning more, so per-share "growth" here should not be read as a sign of improving fundamentals. Second, the new $500m of debt raises the company\'s leverage and fixed interest obligations, meaning the company now carries more financial risk (more amplified sensitivity to any future earnings decline) purely to produce a cosmetic per-share metric improvement — a combination that is a common, legitimate point of scrutiny (and sometimes criticism) around aggressive, debt-funded buyback programs, especially at companies with limited genuine growth opportunities to invest in instead.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Treating EPS growth driven by a shrinking share count (a buyback) as equivalent to REAL earnings growth from the underlying business — the two produce the identical-looking EPS number but come from fundamentally different sources, and only one of them reflects the business actually becoming more profitable.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'What is the immediate mechanical effect of a stock buyback on EPS, holding net income constant?', options: ['EPS falls', 'EPS rises, since the same net income is divided by fewer shares', 'EPS is unaffected', 'EPS becomes negative'], a: 1, why: 'EPS = net income / shares outstanding; reducing the share count (the denominator) mechanically raises EPS even with no change in net income.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'How does a buyback differ mechanically from a dividend, in terms of returning capital to shareholders?', options: ['They are functionally identical in every respect', 'A dividend pays cash directly and proportionally to every shareholder; a buyback reduces share count, raising remaining shareholders\' percentage ownership with no direct cash payment to them', 'A buyback always pays more cash per share than a dividend', 'Dividends reduce share count while buybacks pay cash directly'], a: 1, why: 'Dividends are direct proportional cash payments; buybacks work through the share count instead, with no direct payment to any individual shareholder.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A company has $60m net income and 30m shares (EPS $2.00). It buys back 5m shares with net income unchanged. What is the new EPS?', options: ['$2.00', '$2.40', '$1.71', '$2.20'], a: 1, why: 'New EPS = 60/(30−5) = 60/25 = $2.40.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A large buyback announcement is often read as a POSITIVE signal because:', options: ['It always guarantees the stock will rise', 'Management is spending real cash on the company\'s own stock, a costly and credible signal that they believe it is undervalued', 'Buybacks are always funded with free money from the government', 'It has no signaling content at all'], a: 1, why: 'Because it involves spending real capital rather than making a costless public statement, a buyback is a more credible signal of management\'s genuine view than words alone.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why might a large, debt-funded buyback also be read as a NEGATIVE signal, beyond the leverage risk itself?', options: ['It can suggest management sees no better investment opportunity (growth projects, R&D, acquisitions) worth funding instead of buying back stock', 'Debt-funded buybacks are illegal in most markets', 'It always means the company is about to go bankrupt', 'It has no possible negative interpretation'], a: 0, why: 'A company with abundant attractive growth opportunities would typically prefer to fund those directly rather than return capital via buybacks, so a large buyback can also hint at a thin growth pipeline.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'An analyst sees a company\'s EPS grew 12% year-over-year, but net income was flat. What is the most likely explanation, and why does it matter?', options: ['The tax rate must have fallen to zero', 'A meaningful share buyback likely shrank the share count, mechanically raising EPS with no real earnings growth — this should not be read as a sign of improving underlying business performance', 'This combination is mathematically impossible', 'Depreciation must have increased'], a: 1, why: 'Flat net income with rising EPS is the classic fingerprint of a share-count-driven EPS increase (buybacks), not genuine business growth — an important distinction when assessing whether a company\'s fundamentals are actually improving.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ];

  global.QTL_LESSONS_IB = L;
})(window);
